	title = {Optimising agricultural microgrids for decentralised rural electrification in Cameroon: Current systems and future strategies},
	year = {2026},
	journal = {Energy for Sustainable Development},
	volume = {95},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1016/j.esd.2026.102138},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105050426458?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {School of Geography, Geology and The Environment, Keele University, Staffordshire, United Kingdom; NovaSci, Staffordshire, United Kingdom},
	abstract = {This paper presents an assessment of current agricultural microgrid deployments in rural Cameroon and proposes optimisation strategies to improve affordability, reliability, and productive energy use. The study integrates technical literature, stakeholder interviews, field survey data, and comparative techno-economic modelling to analyse the extent to which existing microgrids support key agricultural operations, including irrigation, cold storage, and crop processing. Results reveal that while hybrid systems such as solar PV-diesel, PV-hydro-biogas, and PV-wind configurations are being adopted, most lack integration with agricultural energy demands and exhibit minimal system automation. Over 70% of cold storage systems remain diesel dependent, reflecting structural and financial constraints despite strong renewable potential. Energy management is constrained by manual controls, poor demand forecasting, and inadequate load balancing. A comparative techno-economic assessment demonstrates that optimised hybrid configurations can reduce effective electricity costs relative to diesel dominant systems while improving renewable penetration under high reliability constraints. Advanced optimisation techniques, including particle swarm optimisation (PSO), Digital Twin (DT), and artificial intelligence (AI) based forecasting, demonstrate significant potential to enhance system efficiency and lower levelised cost of energy (LCOE) when implemented through phased and context-sensitive approaches. Additionally, modular designs and hybrid storage solutions combining lithium-ion batteries and hydrogen offer improved scalability and resilience. The study proposes a context sensitive framework for decentralised microgrid design, aligned with agricultural cycles and community priorities. This work contributes to scalable and locally adapted energy solutions, offering a pathway for integrated rural electrification and agricultural development in Sub-Saharan Africa. © 2026 The Authors},
