	abstract = {Outdoor sports environments often experience severe thermal stress, while sports buildings are energy-intensive. The coupling between dynamic human metabolism and complex outdoor microclimates makes thermal regulation difficult, and effective design tools remain limited. This study proposes a photovoltaic (PV) shading retrofit for semi-outdoor sports spaces and establishes a multi-objective optimization framework integrating thermal comfort, energy performance, and economic efficiency. Future climate data were generated with the Future Weather Generator. Wind simulations provided airflow distributions, followed by Physiologically Equivalent Temperature (PET), Predicted Mean Vote (PMV), and Universal Thermal Climate Index (UTCI) evaluation under different metabolic conditions. Energy performance was assessed with EnergyPlus, covering PV generation and summer cooling demand. Multiple machine-learning surrogate models were compared, and the best (TabPFN) was coupled with the Non-dominated Sorting Genetic Algorithm III (NSGA-III) to obtain Pareto-optimal solutions, interpreted via SHAP and sensitivity analysis. PV shading reduces PET by up to 2.3 °C in core shaded areas; across the Pareto front, PET and UTCI stay tightly clustered (28.1–28.8 °C and 27.2–28.5 °C), while PMV serves for metabolic zoning. Appropriate configurations cut summer cooling demand by about 11,000 kWh, and the optimal design generates roughly 170,000 kWh annually. A 25-year life-cycle evaluation gives a payback of about nine years (98.4% probability of positive net present value), with feasibility resting on combined PV-and-venue revenue. The framework supports low-cost urban retrofit and scalable multi-objective design of sports spaces. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd},
