	abstract = {To accurately manage the increasing penetration of residential solar photovoltaics, Operating Envelopes (OEs) have emerged as an effective tool for optimizing energy exports while maintaining network integrity. OEs, which are time-varying export limits at the customer connection point, rely heavily on forecast data of customer demand, reactive power, and voltage at the secondary side of the distribution transformer, yet Low Voltage (LV) forecast remains a significant challenge. While deterministic methods like Linear Regression and machine learning approaches such as Random Forest or Neural Networks have been widely explored, they struggle to capture the uncertainty in house demand. Probabilistic forecasting offers a way to quantify this uncertainty, expanding OE accuracy by using a more information-rich probabilistic approach. This study introduces a probabilistic forecasting approach for Monte Carlo simulations under three generation sampling approaches, one of which is proposed by this paper, for OE calculation and evaluates its performance against five deterministic forecasting methods. Using real Australian smart meter data, the study assesses forecast accuracy under different Photovoltaic (PV) penetration levels and flexible customer participation rates across modern (lower impedance) and conventional (higher impedance) LV networks. Results show that deterministic machine learning models, particularly an Ensemble-based approach, perform well under moderate PV penetration in modern networks. However, under higher PV penetration or in conventional networks, probabilistic methods substantially improve OE effectiveness, albeit with higher computational requirements. The findings highlight the importance of advanced forecasting techniques in enhancing OE efficacy under challenging conditions, ensuring network operation within technical constraints. © 2026 The Authors.},
