	abstract = {Doping engineering offers a promising strategy to enhance the performance of inorganic perovskite solar cells (PSCs), yet identifying optimal B-site dopants remains time-consuming and resource-intensive. Notably, a systematic investigation into B-site cation doping for inorganic PSCs using machine learning (ML) has been lacking. In this study, we introduce a novel approach that integrates experimental and computational data through a data augmentation framework tailored for small datasets. This strategy enables the efficient identification of high-performance B-site dopants for inorganic PSCs. We establish a strong correlation between key physical parameters of B-site cations, such as ionic radius, valence state, dopant type, concentration, and the photovoltaic performance of the resulting devices. Using a data augmentation strategy based on fuzzy c-means clustering combined with a double-point crossover operator (FCM-C2), we generated synthetic datasets that exhibited an 86.5% alignment with experimental data across all evaluated features. Among various ML models, the extreme gradient boosting regressor (XGBR) demonstrated superior performance, achieving a correlation coefficient (r) of 0.94 and exhibiting high accuracy in prediction and extrapolation. This work highlights the potential of ML-driven data augmentation in accelerating dopant discovery and design, and suggests that a broader search of chemically relevant B-site elements could unlock new pathways toward high-efficiency inorganic perovskite solar cells. Our framework paves the way for harnessing the compositional diversity of halide perovskites by bridging experimental insights with computational efficiency. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.},
