	abstract = {Hybrid renewable energy system (HRES), which integrate multiple generation sources such as solar photovoltaics, wind turbines, and energy storage systems (ESSs), are increasingly recognized as foundational components in the transition toward low-carbon and resilient energy infrastructures. In this context, artificial intelligence (AI) has emerged as a critical enabler for managing the complexity and variability inherent in HRES operation and planning. This systematic review synthesizes the state of research on AI-driven decision-making frameworks applied to HRES, with a particular focus on machine learning, deep learning, and metaheuristic optimization techniques. Following a rigorous screening process, 270 peer-reviewed publications spanning 2010 to 2025 were analyzed across dimensions of system design, forecasting, real-time control, and lifecycle optimization. The findings reveal that AI technologies significantly enhance forecasting accuracy, improve scheduling efficiency, and contribute to intelligent fault detection and energy dispatch strategies. Applications of deep learning architectures, such as LSTM, GRU, and CNN, demonstrate superior performance in modeling nonlinear temporal dynamics, while reinforcement learning enables adaptive control under uncertainty. Despite these advancements, the review also identifies critical limitations, including high computational demands, limited transferability across geographies, data scarcity in real-world deployments, and insufficient attention to socio-economic and environmental dimensions. By consolidating methodological trends and performance benchmarks, this review offers a research roadmap that advocates for the development of interpretable, scalable, and energy efficient AI solutions. It emphasizes the need for standardized datasets, cross-sector integration, and inclusive design approaches that align with broader energy justice and climate resilience goals. The review contributes a foundational reference for researchers, system designers, and policymakers, positioning AI enhanced HRES as a central pillar in achieving global decarbonization targets. Copyright © 2026. Published by Elsevier Ltd.},
