	abstract = {Developing efficient, non-toxic perovskite solar cells (PSCs) is vital for sustainable photovoltaics (PVs), yet most lead (Pb)-free materials still fall short of Pb-based performance. To overcome this problem, we present a novel heterojunction Al/FTO/TiO<sub>2</sub>/CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>3</sub>SnI<sub>3</sub>/Sb<sub>2</sub>S<sub>3</sub>/Au solar cell structure and investigated using the SCAPS-1D simulation. In this numerical study, CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>3</sub>SnI<sub>3</sub>-based PSC integrated with various organic and inorganic charge transport layers, TiO<sub>2</sub> as electron transport layer (ETL), and Sb<sub>2</sub>S<sub>3</sub> as hole transport layer (HTL) significantly outperforms organic alternatives (PTAA, P<sub>3</sub>HT, C<sub>60</sub>). The proposed PSC achieves a simulated power conversion efficiency (PCE) of 32.88% under ideal conditions, with a V<sub>oc</sub> of 1.08 V, J<sub>sc</sub> of 34.39 mA/cm², and FF of 88.26%. Additionally, the PV characteristics of the proposed PSC are studied by varying different absorber parameters such as thickness, doping concentration, defect density, and optimized conditions are identified for maximum PV output. Temperature analysis, metal work function, and series/shunt resistance studies are also evaluated. Moreover, eleven machine learning (ML) models are trained on 29,514 unique SCAPS-1D-generated device configurations using eight perovskite absorber parameters. Among them, XGBoost achieved the highest predictive accuracy (R² = 0.9992, RMSE = 0.0686, MAE = 0.0454, RRMSE = 0.21%) among the eight absorber parameters. Feature importance and SHAP analysis from the XGBoost model identified electron affinity as the most influential parameter with 23.60% relative importance. Pearson correlation findings also align with these results. This integrated simulation and ML framework establishes an approach to designing high-efficiency, non-toxic Pb-free PSCs in sustainable photovoltaic technology. © 2026 Published by Elsevier Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/},
