	abstract = {The urgent need to mitigate climate change and enhance energy security has accelerated the global adoption of solar energy, with photovoltaic (PV) capacity growing at an unprecedented rate. However, the inherent intermittency of solar power—which typically causes grid instability when solar penetration exceeds 15–20% of total electricity generation—poses a significant stability challenge for conventional power grids. This review examines recent advances in three high-efficiency solar cell technologies: perovskite solar cells (laboratory efficiency >26%), multi-junction tandem solar cells) power conversion efficiencies exceeding 33% under one-sun illumination (, and bifacial solar cells. These technologies are discussed alongside smart grid systems, microgrids, and innovative energy storage solutions. Furthermore, when such PV systems are combined with artificial intelligence-based forecasting and dynamic demand response, the intermittency problem can be effectively mitigated, as demonstrated in the literature reviewed here. Finally, economic policies (including feed-in tariffs, tax incentives, and carbon pricing) and environmental life-cycle assessments of advanced PV technologies are discussed, as these factors are critical for successful grid-connected deployment. © 2026 The Author(s)},
