	abstract = {This study presents a framework based on the Energy-Land-Water-Air (ELWA) nexus to determine an efficient method for generating electricity using the region's available solar energy resources. The proposed framework has been developed to reduce water consumption and CO<sub>2</sub> emissions from energy production in metropolises, and it consists of six steps. The framework follows a structured process that evaluates current power plants and their electricity outputs. It then assesses water consumption, evaluates CO<sub>2</sub> emissions from electricity production, and identifies suitable areas for solar farm development. After gathering key data, including energy demand, direct normal solar irradiance, aerosol optical depth, and land use, the framework optimizes solar panel configurations to enhance efficiency and reduce costs. Finally, the framework estimates reductions in water consumption and CO<sub>2</sub> emissions and the cost of meeting the city's electricity needs through solar energy. The proposed framework was applied to the Tehran metropolis as a case study. The results show that, compared to fossil fuel-based electricity generation, optimized solar power generation can reduce water consumption by 72 % from 429 to 118 L/MWh and CO<sub>2</sub> emissions by 92 % from 401 to 33 kg/MWh, while increasing the levelized cost of electricity (LCOE) from 0.040 to 0.053 USD/kWh. In addition, modeling results showed that air pollution reduces solar power generation by 25 %. The ELWA nexus framework enables data-driven policymaking for urban energy transition to solar energy by identifying optimal locations that balance land costs and solar potential, and quantifying the water consumption and CO<sub>2</sub> emissions reductions achievable with solar energy. Copyright © 2026. Published by Elsevier B.V.},
