	abstract = {The penetration of solar power challenges grid stability due to its intermittent generation. Accurate ultra-short-term forecasting is essential for accommodating and integrating the increasing solar production. This paper proposes a data reconstruction-based deep probabilistic network (DRDPN) for probabilistic solar power forecasting. We introduce an adaptive complete ensemble empirical mode decomposition with adaptive noise (ACEEMDAN) to accommodate non-stationary photovoltaic (PV) time-series. This serves as necessary preprocessing that facilitates ultra-short-term forecasting. Then, we develop a two-stage post-processing framework that reconstructs the time-series into frequency-differentiated components. In particular, this framework implements spurious-mode consolidation via orthogonality thresholding and dual-metric frequency classification, ensuring cross-dataset dimensional consistency. Furthermore, we design a heterogeneous multi-network architecture to extract component-specific features and enable adaptive cross-modal fusion. Comprehensive experiments across different real-world PV systems validate DRDPN’s superiority. The results demonstrate that DRDPN achieves 4.57% and 57.10% improvements in R<sup>2</sup> and nMAE over baselines, respectively. DRDPN also attains superior continuous ranked probability score (CRPS) and Winkler score (WS) across multiple datasets, exhibiting exceptional stability under highly volatile power generation conditions. Computational analysis reveals minimal resource consumption, indicating its suitability for resource-constrained deployment. © 2026 International Solar Energy Society.},
