	abstract = {Optimal scheduling of virtual power plants (VPPs) plays a critical role in coordinating multiple distributed energy resources and improving economic performance. However, the fluctuating characteristics of renewable energy sources and the complexity of multi-energy systems introduce significant operational challenges for conventional scheduling approaches. To address this issue, this paper proposes a deep reinforcement learning-based multi-objective scheduling method for VPPs under uncertain operating conditions of wind and solar power. First, a multi-objective optimal scheduling model for a VPP is developed. The scheduling problem is then formulated as a reinforcement learning task with enhanced representations of the state space, action space, and reward function. Subsequently, an improved Deep Deterministic Policy Gradient algorithm is adopted to obtain the optimal scheduling strategy. Notably, the proposed method reduces the reliance on explicit forecasting models and future information. Instead, it enables real-time dispatch decisions through policies trained on historical data, thereby maintaining effective scheduling performance under renewable fluctuation environments. Finally, Case study results demonstrate that, under high-volatility renewable energy scenarios, the proposed method achieves a 4.17% reduction in operating cost and a 2.46% reduction in carbon emissions compared with DQN, and a 7.82% reduction in operating cost and a 1.22% reduction in carbon emissions compared with the robust optimization method, while also exhibiting faster convergence and improved stability compared with benchmark approaches. © 2026 Published by Elsevier Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/},
