	abstract = {To fully leverage the value of historical photovoltaic data for power forecasting, a method incorporating scenario reperformance features is proposed. First, an evaluation index is introduced to measure the similarity between future and historical photovoltaic output scenarios. This index comprehensively considers both amplitude and phase angle constraints. Based on this index, key features are selected from historical data to support real-time decision-making in the power forecasting model. Next, a spatio-temporal feature extraction model based on dynamic adaptive graph neural networks is proposed. The long-term stability of photovoltaic output is represented by a static graph, while dynamic changes in spatial correlation are captured through an adaptive graph structure. Finally, multi-step forecasting for multiple photovoltaic power outputs is achieved using an improved multi-task learning algorithm. Using operational data from a photovoltaic cluster in Jilin Province, China, the proposed model achieves an NRMSE of 0.0743 for ultra-short-term 4-hour-ahead forecasting, indicating a relatively low forecasting error and demonstrating its effectiveness. © 2026 The Authors. Published by Elsevier Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/},
