	abstract = {Reliable short-term operation of a renewable-rich smart grid requires accurate multistep forecasting together with explicit treatment of network disturbances. This study therefore focuses on six-hour-ahead joint forecasting of wind generation, solar generation, and electricity demand, with grid-state quantities used to assess physical consistency and a fault classifier used as a downstream resilience module. A physics-informed, fault-aware Graph Neural Network (GNN)–Transformer framework is proposed: the GNN learns topology-dependent spatial relationships among buses and grid components, whereas the Transformer captures temporal dependencies and seasonal variation over the preceding 24 h. Power-balance, voltage-bound, line-capacity, renewable-limit, and storage-consistency residuals regularize the forecasting objective, while fault-aware graph updates respond to line, generator, sensor, and communication disturbances. The evaluation uses a one-year, 8760-h simulation benchmark constructed around the IEEE 39-bus system; the revised manuscript does not claim an untraceable utility field-measurement component. Using one consistent result definition, the framework achieves an overall target-average MAE of 1.96%, pooled RMSE of 2.84%, and forecasting accuracy of 97.12%; the average MAE under fault conditions is 2.17%. Relative to the strongest physics-unaware GNN–Transformer baseline, pooled RMSE is reduced by 9.0%, while power-balance residual, voltage violations, and energy imbalance are reduced by 46.2%, 46.8%, and 21.7%, respectively. These results support improved numerical forecasting, physical consistency, and fault-resilient energy-management performance without claiming exact AC-feasibility certification. © 2026 The Author(s).},
