	abstract = {Accurate short-term net load forecasting is crucial for reliable power system dispatching and operation. To address the forecasting difficulty caused by photovoltaic output fluctuations and typhoon weather, this paper proposes a hybrid indirect forecasting framework based on a full-convolutional temporal convolutional network (FTCN) and a bidirectional long short-term memory network (BiLSTM). The FTCN module is used to extract multivariate temporal features from load, photovoltaic and meteorological time-series data, while the BiLSTM module further captures nonlinear temporal dependencies. Meteorological variables are selected through correlation analysis and incorporated into the corresponding load and photovoltaic forecasting branches. Validation is performed using measured load, photovoltaic and meteorological data from a coastal region, including seven real typhoon events during 2023–2024. Comparative experiments under normal and typhoon weather conditions show that the proposed FTCN-BiLSTM model achieves lower forecasting errors than the baseline models considered in this study, indicating its potential for short-term net load forecasting under typhoon-affected conditions. © 2026 The Authors. Published by Elsevier Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/},
