	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105042464327?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {UCASE Software Engineering Research Group, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, University of Cadiz, Avda. de la Universidad de Cádiz 10, Cádiz, Puerto Real, 11519, Spain},
	abstract = {Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML) are increasingly adopted in Industrial IoT (IIoT) and renewable energy systems, yet conventional MLOps pipelines still provide limited guarantees regarding the integrity, traceability, and post-hoc verifiability of model execution. This limitation is especially relevant in solar-plant operations, where forecast-driven alarms may influence maintenance prioritization and forensic analysis after incidents. In this work, we define Immutable AI as an MLOps pattern in which model artifacts, inference inputs, and selected outputs are cryptographically fingerprinted off-chain and anchored on-chain to create a tamper-evident audit trail. Based on this concept, we propose a hybrid framework that combines an LSTM model for photovoltaic power forecasting with Ethereum smart contracts for audit registration. The framework hashes serialized model artifacts, input snapshots, and inference records, and links them to MLflow run metadata to support version-aware verification. The prototype is assessed using a real-world photovoltaic dataset containing approximately 682,000 raw measurements across multiple installations, with detailed forecasting experiments conducted on a high-quality reference installation after preprocessing. The forecasting module achieves an RMSE of 0.1567 kW on the selected test set, while the blockchain layer demonstrates feasible anomaly-event notarization on Sepolia under a hash-only storage strategy. Rather than claiming full predictive-maintenance validation, the paper positions the contribution as an auditable forecasting and anomaly-logging framework that strengthens integrity, provenance, and reproducibility for maintenance assurance workflows. The study also formalizes the threat model, discusses privacy and trust assumptions, and analyzes latency, cost, and scalability trade-offs relevant to industrial deployment. © 2026 The Author(s).},
