	affiliations = {School of New Energy and Power Engineering, Lanzhou Jiaotong University, Lanzhou, 730070, China},
	abstract = {With the rapid expansion of the photovoltaic (PV) industry into arid and semi-arid regions, dust deposition on module surfaces has emerged as a major bottleneck, constraining power generation efficiency and increasing operation and maintenance costs. However, existing approaches remain limited in detection accuracy, interpretability, and adaptability under diverse environmental conditions. To address these challenges, this study proposes a dust recognition framework integrating HSV color space features with a LightGBM-based adaptive thresholding model. The method employs a multi-channel joint discrimination mechanism: a fixed threshold in the H channel, machine-learning-based threshold prediction in the S channel, and peak–valley structural analysis in the V channel. This design ensures robust performance under complex illumination and background conditions. Furthermore, a standardized pollution-level classification scheme is established based on the estimated dust coverage, enabling a direct link between detection outcomes and PV system operation and maintenance requirements. Experiments show that the proposed method performs well on both a self-constructed dataset and real outdoor scenarios, achieving an intersection-over-union (IoU) of 96% and an F1-score of 98%; on the same test set, it outperforms Otsu thresholding (IoU 53%, F1-score 69%) and a U-Net baseline (IoU 93%, F1-score 95%). These findings not only improve detection accuracy and practical applicability but also provide an actionable pathway for intelligent operation and maintenance of PV power plants. © 2026 International Solar Energy Society.},
