	doi = {10.1016/j.apenergy.2026.128676},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047338589?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {School of Metallurgical and Energy Engineering, Kunming University of Science and Technology, Kunming, 650093, China; State Key Laboratory of Complex Nonferrous Metal Resources Clean Utilization, School of Metallurgical and Energy Engineering, Kunming University of Science and Technology, Kunming, 650093, China; Silicon Industry and Engineering Research Center of Yunnan Province, School of Metallurgical and Energy Engineering, Kunming University of Science and Technology, Kunming, 650093, China; School of Engineering, Yunnan University, Kunming, 650091, China},
	abstract = {The rapid expansion of photovoltaic (PV) installations has made dust deposition a major factor limiting power generation, reliability, and economic performance. Soiling reduces optical transmittance, decreases output, and accelerates hotspot formation and material degradation. Consequently, research has focused on soiling mechanisms, cleaning technologies, and frequency optimization. This review synthesizes recent advances in PV module cleaning frequency optimization and proposes an integrated perspective linking dust formation, cleaning methods, and scheduling strategies. Dust deposition mechanisms are analyzed considering environmental conditions, contaminant properties, and PV system configuration. Cleaning approaches—including natural, manual, automated, and preventive cleaning techniques—are compared in terms of technical features and application scenarios. The evolution of cleaning frequency optimization is examined, highlighting the shift from fixed-cycle empirical schedules to threshold-based strategies and further to predictive, adaptive models enabled by IoT and artificial intelligence. This transition reflects a broader move from experience-driven to adaptive and data-driven O&M management. Optimal cleaning frequency emerges as a multi-factor problem influenced by environmental variability, dust characteristics, cleaning technologies, and economic constraints. Future research should target real-time data-driven optimization, durable anti-soiling materials, and integrated intelligent O&M systems to improve energy yield and reduce lifecycle costs. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd},
