	abstract = {In modern life, increasing environmental pollution from fossil-fuel use and the depletion of non-renewable energy resources have intensified the global shift toward sustainable energy solutions. Among renewable technologies, solar power has emerged as one of the most significant options for clean electricity generation and climate-resilient energy systems. Using solar energy requires the use of photovoltaic systems. We considered nine candidate factors of temperature, wind speed, relative humidity, cloudiness, sea-level pressure, sunshine hours, precipitation, elevation, and geographic coordinates that influence rooftop solar potential, and removed those that were strongly correlated. We used layers of elevation, temperature, latitude, and wind speed (averaged from 1999 to 2023) as independent features. The dependent layers, diffuse horizontal irradiation (DHI) and direct horizontal irradiation (BHI), were derived from Solargis Atlas. The shading effect was applied to calculate global horizontal radiation (GHI) on building surfaces. Machine learning methods included support vector regression (SVR), multilayer perception (MLP), multivariate adaptive regression spline (MARS), classification and regression tree (CART), random forest methods (RF), gradient boosting machine (GBM), and extreme gradient boosting (XGBoost). Among them, the best method with the highest accuracy in predicting DHI and BHI was identified, and features were ranked using the method of Recursive Feature Elimination (RFE) in the order of temperature, wind speed, latitude, and elevation in estimating BHI, and latitude, temperature, wind speed, and elevation were determined in the estimation of DHI. XGBoost method with Coefficient of determination (R<sup>2</sup>), Root Mean Square Error (RMSE), and Mean Absolute Error (MAE) with respectively 0.986, 0.065 and 0.043 were chosen to estimate BHI and with 0.963, 0.034 and 0.028 were chosen to estimate DHI. © 2026 The Authors.},
