	abstract = {Accurate forecasting of wind speed and photovoltaic (PV) power is essential for reliable renewable energy integration, dispatch scheduling, and grid stability, but remains challenging due to strong temporal volatility and site-level spatial heterogeneity. While Large Language Models (LLMs) excel in natural language processing and vision, their direct application fails to capture the intricate spatio-temporal dependencies inherent in meteorological and power systems. To address this, we propose STS-MambaGPT, a two-stage spatio-temporal forecasting framework that incorporates a Graph Attention Network (GAT) to encode spatial relationships between turbines and photovoltaic plants via a Dynamic Time Warping (DTW)-based adjacency matrix, thereby capturing lagged similarity patterns associated with atmospheric transport. For learning long term dependencies and temporal differences in data, we proposed the stacked Mamba encoder. The framework operates in two phases: (1) an alignment stage where a lightweight NanoGPT is pretrained on domain-specific sequences, and (2) a fine-tuning stage where multi-scale convolutions, the graph-attention module, and a novel additive cross-attention module extract and synchronize spatio-temporal features, which are then refined by the Mamba encoder. Extensive experiments on publicly available, high-fidelity simulated and measured renewable energy datasets demonstrate that STS-MambaGPT outperforms state-of-the-art models, including Transformer-based, spatio-temporal graph neural network, and pretrained LLM-based approaches, achieving a coefficient of determination of 0.872 (a 2.5% improvement over the strongest baseline), and reducing the mean absolute error to 1.52 (a 3.2% improvement). Crucially, residual distribution and Bland–Altman agreement analyses confirm the model’s enhanced reliability, showing reduced bias and tighter error dispersion, a direct benefit of its structure-aware design. © 2026 Published by Elsevier Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/},
