	abstract = {The intermittent and weather-dependent nature of solar and wind energy output poses considerable hurdles to the increasing integration of these sources into current energy grids. Stability and the efficient use of energy resources depend on accurate forecasting. This study aims to develop a neural network model for forecasting renewable energy in South Africa to reduce reliance on coal and other fossil fuels and boost energy efficiency to meet the maximum potential energy demand. The research employs supervised machine-learning methods to forecast energy output. The simulation offers a framework for analysing the behaviour and efficacy of components essential for the design and implementation of hybrid energy generation forecasts in South Africa. The MATLAB toolbox was employed to analyse key matrices, including root mean square errors, mean square errors, and regression correlations, to forecast power generation statistics in South Africa and assess the accuracy of the predictions. The PV model demonstrated exceptional performance, achieving an R-value of 0.99012 in training and 1.000 in both validation and testing, reflecting high accuracy. The study's findings reveal that >80% of South Africa's plateau possesses adequate wind potential to achieve a 30% average annual load factor. Additionally, the country's photovoltaic (PV) capabilities and weather conditions are favourable for generating competitively priced solar and wind energy on an international scale. The results also show that dispersing power plants over a large area can result in consistent power production from wind and solar PV systems. © 2026 The Author(s). Published by Elsevier B.V. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/},
