	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105050777042?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Department of Industrial and Mechanical Engineering, Lebanese American University, Lebanon},
	abstract = {This paper proposes a physics-based and machine-learning-based framework to predict the optimum tilt angle, β, and azimuth angle, γ, for fixed monofacial and bifacial photovoltaic systems at yearly, seasonal, and monthly scales. First, a physical irradiation model was used with NSRDB Meteosat Prime Meridian data, location, albedo, PV type, and time period to generate the optimal orientation and corresponding maximum irradiation. These physically generated targets were then used to train supervised ML models, allowing rapid prediction without repeating the full irradiation search for each new case. Four regression models were tested for β prediction, while γ was predicted through a three-stage framework: zero/nonzero classification, east/west classification, and regression of the azimuth magnitude. The results show that β is highly predictable at all time scales, with the best models giving MAE values of 1.6°, 1.7°, and 1.9° and R<sup>2</sup> values of 0.968, 0.981, and 0.978 for yearly, seasonal, and monthly prediction, respectively. By contrast, γ prediction was strongly time-scale dependent. In particular, the yearly east/west azimuth-direction classifier was non-functional, with only 8.0% accuracy and 52.1% balanced accuracy, showing that the annual azimuth direction could not be reliably learned from the selected input variables. Seasonal and monthly azimuth prediction were more informative, but remained less accurate than tilt prediction. The analysis of the optimized dataset also shows that latitude is the main driver of β, whereas γ is more sensitive to time scale, albedo, and PV type. Finally, testing on 50 unseen cities confirmed strong generalization, with predicted annual optimum irradiation remaining very close to the calculated reference values. © 2026 The Author(s)},
