	affiliations = {Faculty of Electrical and Computer Engineering, Tarbiat Modares University, Tehran, Iran},
	abstract = {The rapid proliferation of electric vehicles (EVs) imposes major operational challenges on distribution networks, including feeder overloading, peak current violations, and increased charging costs. This paper proposes a cyber–physical, model-free energy management framework for coordinated multi-station EV charging under explicit feeder current constraints. The proposed architecture integrates real-time sensing, bidirectional communication, and a centralized energy management system (EMS) embedding a tabular Q-learning agent for adaptive charging-current control and inter-station power coordination. The energy management problem is formulated as a Markov decision process incorporating electricity tariffs, photovoltaic (PV) generation forecasts, EV charging demand, battery state-of-charge, and feeder current limits. Unlike model-dependent optimization and computationally intensive deep reinforcement learning approaches, the proposed framework provides an interpretable and computationally efficient solution with low runtime complexity and modest memory requirements for the investigated case study. The approach is validated using real-world EV charging demand data from Austria on a four-station charging network. Simulation results demonstrate approximately a 23% reduction in peak feeder current and an 18% reduction in average charging cost compared with uncontrolled charging. The present implementation assumes reliable EMS-to-station communication with negligible latency and packet loss; therefore, the reported results should be interpreted as a proof-of-concept validation of the investigated four-station cyber–physical charging network rather than full validation for large-scale deployment. © 2026 The Author(s). Published by Elsevier Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC license. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/},
	author_keywords = {Data-driven energy management; Model-free control; Multi-station EV charging; Photovoltaic integration; Reinforcement learning},
