	abstract = {We present a machine learning guided design approach for mixed-cation ABX<sub>3</sub> perovskites with monovalent copper (Cu<sup>+</sup>) as the A-site cation candidate for high photovoltaic performance and stability. First, we generated a dataset comprised of various perovskite compositions with materials properties such as electronic structure, carrier transport, and structural stability. Random forest predictors trained to accurately predict (R<sup>2</sup>>0.85) metrics of power conversion efficiency (PCE) and stability. A feature importance analysis indicated that the dominant predictors were bandgap (for PCE) and octahedral distortion (for stability). Our models recognised compositions with optimal ratios of Cu<sup>+</sup>/Cs<sup>+</sup> mixing at the A-site, allowing maximum performance and stability. The most-performing compositions involved Cu<sup>+</sup> content in the 4–8 unit range, especially when with Cs<sup>+</sup> and Rb<sup>+</sup>. Orbital overlap between Cu<sup>+</sup> and the MCl<sub>6</sub> octahedron was found to significantly enhance carrier mobility and reduce decomposition energy. Multi-objective optimization identified promising candidates with optimal properties, e.g., Cu8Pb1Sr3Cl24Br24I24 with high stability (0.92) and yet competitive PCE (0.25%). These results provide a foundation for designing stable, high-performance Cu<sup>+</sup>-based perovskites and demonstrate the success of machine learning in accelerating the discovery of new photovoltaic materials. © 2026 The Authors},
