	abstract = {This paper presents a rolling horizon-based energy exchange in a building-to-building (B2B) grid using a game theory method. The system combines the predictive capability of long-short-term memory (LSTM) based machine learning forecasting with game theory-based energy exchange decisions, enabling informed energy exchange decisions between participating buildings. A customized LSTM model is implemented to improve forecasting, utilizing adaptable hyperparameters, lookback windows, and a 24-hour forecast horizon to accommodate individual building photovoltaic (PV) generation and load energy profiles. The system formulates the energy exchange between the buildings in a non-cooperative Stackelberg game. The proposed model is analyzed, and a comparison is carried out for a game theory-based model without a forecast, with an LSTM-based forecast, and with a perfect forecast, considering four buildings with PV generation and load profile measurements from Barcelona, Spain. The proposed model with an LSTM-based and perfect forecast has a percentage cost saving of 2.67 % to 28.63 % and 3.33 % to 58.82 %, respectively. The sensitivity of the proposed model to energy storage capacity change and utility grid price variations is also studied. The proposed model demonstrates scalability by efficiently optimizing energy exchange with a higher number of buildings with optimal execution times, showcasing its ability to handle larger, more complex networks while maintaining reasonable computational performance. © 2025 The Authors.},
