	abstract = {To overcome the inherent volatility and instability of photovoltaic (PV) power generation, ensure the stability of the power grid upon the integration of PV stations, and reduce energy waste such as power curtailment, this paper proposes a hybrid PV power forecasting model composed of Hierarchical Decomposition (HD), Temporal Convolutional Networks (TCN), Gated Recurrent Units (GRU), and an Attention mechanism. The proposed model first employs Wavelet Threshold Denoising (WTD) to preprocess the original power data, while utilizing the Pearson Correlation Coefficient (PCC) to screen out key meteorological factors. Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) is then used to select the most influential frequency components as the dominant periodic components. A Gaussian sliding weighted average method is applied to decompose the minor components, obtaining secondary trends and residual sequences. Furthermore, TCN is utilized to extract deep features from the periodic components, secondary trends, residual sequences, and meteorological features. The Attention mechanism is subsequently applied to fuse these extracted features, and finally, the fused sequence is fed into the GRU for prediction. Simulation experiments were conducted on the Australian DKASC dataset. The results demonstrate that the HD-TCN-Attention-GRU model outperforms other models across multiple evaluation metrics; notably, the R<sup>2</sup> value reaches 0.9704 in summer, thereby verifying the accuracy and stability of the proposed model.  © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
