	abstract = {The prediction of photovoltaic power is crucial for the safe and stable operation of the power grid. This paper proposes a collaborative prediction framework that integrates multivariate adaptive fuzzy granulation and distributed probability modeling to address the strong intermittency and volatility of photovoltaic power generation. The front-end dynamically extracts local features through adaptive fuzzy granulation, and integrates multiple external variables (irradiance, temperature, humidity, etc.) and multi-scale time information (hour/day/month encoding) to construct physically interpretable enhanced inputs; The back-end uses Distribution Autoformer to generate parameterized distributions and key quantiles through dual branch probability output network. After the front-end features are input into the back-end, the prediction error is back-propagated through loss function, providing feedback to guide feature expression and achieving end-to-end collaborative optimization. The model utilizes quantile crossover penalty terms and post-processing constraints to achieve zero-quantile crossover, with an inference speed of approximately 5.3 ms per sample. Experiments in two regions with significant climate differences showed that the model achieved excellent point prediction accuracy (with mean absolute percentage errors of 18.53% and 31.71%, respectively) while maintaining zero-quantile crossover and good probability coverage. © 2026 International Solar Energy Society.},
