	abstract = {Recent developments in Graphene–MXene hybrid perovskite solar films have demonstrated strong potential for next-generation photovoltaic systems due to their enhanced charge transport and thermal dissipation characteristics. However, existing computational and artificial intelligence approaches are limited in capturing three-dimensional atomic symmetries, quantum-level interactions, and coupled thermal degradation behavior, leading to inaccurate performance prediction and inefficient material optimization. To address these challenges, this work proposes a novel AI-driven framework integrating an E(3)-Equivariant Neural Field (EENF) with Quantum-behaved Aquila Optimization (QAO) for intelligent design and multi-objective optimization of hybrid perovskite materials. The proposed EENF model performs symmetry-preserving atomic graph learning by encoding rotational and translational invariance directly into the neural architecture, enabling physically consistent prediction of power conversion efficiency, thermal conductivity, and degradation rate. The predicted material responses are subsequently optimized using QAO, a quantum-inspired adaptive optimization algorithm that combines probabilistic quantum jumps with Aquila-based exploration and exploitation strategies to efficiently search complex material composition spaces. In addition, a self-regulation feedback mechanism dynamically adjusts Graphene–MXene ratios to minimize thermal instability and degradation during operation. Experimental simulations conducted in Python demonstrated superior predictive capability with R² = 0.97 and MAE = 0.23, outperforming conventional RF, ANN, SVM, and CNN models. The optimized hybrid configuration achieved 25.9% power conversion efficiency, approximately 15% reduction in thermal rise, and nearly 28% improvement in operational stability compared with baseline perovskite systems. The proposed EENF–QAO framework establishes an explainable, scalable, and thermally adaptive pathway for autonomous discovery of high-efficiency photovoltaic materials for sustainable energy applications. © 2026 The Authors.},
