	abstract = {PSCs, or perovskite solar cells, are a next-generation photovoltaic technology that is gaining popularity due to their unique properties. The optimum designs of the In<sub>2</sub>AgSbCl<sub>6</sub> double perovskite-based solar cells are investigated using a combination of density functional theory (DFT) and SCAPS-1D. In this research, we executed a DFT analysis to evaluate and assess the structural, electronic, and optical properties of the In<sub>2</sub>AgSbCl<sub>6</sub> absorber. The electronic band gap (E<sub>g</sub>) of the In<sub>2</sub>AgSbCl<sub>6</sub> absorber was 1.321 eV and 2.582 eV, respectively, using the GGA and Tb-mBJ approaches of DFT. This study aimed to examine the In<sub>2</sub>AgSbCl<sub>6</sub>-based perovskite absorber material and model the outcomes using the SCAPS-1D simulation program. Different configurations of In<sub>2</sub>AgSbCl<sub>6</sub>-based solar cells were designed using an ITO substrate, ZnS, PCBM, ZnSe, and C<sub>60</sub> as Electron Transport Layer (ETL) components, In<sub>2</sub>AgSbCl<sub>6</sub> as the absorber, MoS<sub>2</sub> as the Hole Transport Layer (HTL), and Pt as the back contact. The thickness of the absorber and the ETL have a big impact on the device's performance. Additionally, the comprehensive analysis was conducted to evaluate the impact of several factors, including the absorber defect density, shunt and series resistance, temperature, Mott-Schottky behavior, capacitance, recombination, generation rates, J-V (current–voltage density) characteristics, and quantum efficiency (QE). In<sub>2</sub>AgSbCl<sub>6</sub>-based solar cell structures are investigated four best optimized combinations, and power conversion efficiencies (PCE) of devices optimized with 27.16%, 27.13%, 27.08%, and 26.31% of ETLs and HTLs combination. ITO/ZnS/In<sub>2</sub>AgSbCl<sub>6</sub>/MoS<sub>2</sub>/Pt displays the best performance, with a power conversion efficiency of 27.16%, a current density (J<sub>SC</sub>) of 34