	abstract = {This study presents an artificial intelligence (AI) and simulation-based approach to design optimal microgrid systems for the Pegatan and Kadok locations in Indonesia. Multiple AI models—Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), Random Forest, Histogram-Based Gradient Boosting (HistGB), Convolutional Neural Network (CNN), and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) were applied for renewable resource forecasting using historical data from 1984 to 2019 for training and 2020–2023 for testing. Furthermore, the model's performance was assessed using cross-validation, statistical tests, and error metric evaluations. The findings reveal that the LSTM model achieved the highest accuracy and demonstrated superior performance in capturing wind and solar patterns compared to other models. Subsequently, diesel generators (DGs), photovoltaics (PV), wind turbines (WTs), lead-acid (LA), and lithium-ion (LI) batteries are employed to simulate microgrid configurations using historical and predicted data. The results identified PV–DG–LI combinations as the most cost-effective solution for both historical and forecasted scenarios. At Pegatan, the Net Present Cost (NPC) and Levelized Cost of Energy (LCOE) were $1,776,010 & $0.360 per kilowatt-hour (kWh) for historical data, and $1,758,010 & $0.360/kWh for forecast data. The respective values for Kadok were $1,174,044 & $0.367/kWh for historical data, and $1,173,314 & $0.367/kWh for forecasted data. The forecast results indicate that the NPC difference was below 1.5%, with no discernible change in LCOE relative to historical outcomes. In practical terms, even minor variations in NPC can trigger significant financial impacts on large-scale renewable projects. This study shows that artificial intelligence-based renewable resource forecasting can effectively reduce uncertainty in techno-economic assessments and support cost-effective microgrid designs. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.},
