	abstract = {The performance of photovoltaic (PV) cells is adversely affected by heat accumulation during solar irradiation, leading to a reduction in electrical efficiency as the cell temperature increases. Although photovoltaic thermal (PV/T) systems have been widely employed to alleviate this limitation, improving energy quality and reducing thermodynamic irreversibility remain important challenges. The present study experimentally investigates the thermodynamic performance of flat, dimpled, and dimpled–protruded PV/T configurations and develops a machine learning-based framework for performance prediction and optimisation. Experiments were conducted over a Reynolds number range of 2,796 to 10,121, and the system performance was evaluated using second-law thermodynamic parameters, including exergy efficiency, entropy generation, and the thermo-exergetic performance factor. An artificial neural network (ANN) model was subsequently developed to predict the thermodynamic performance of the investigated configurations. The results demonstrate that the dimpled–protruded configuration consistently outperforms the flat and dimpled configurations owing to enhanced flow mixing and improved heat transfer. The exergy efficiency ranges from 17.40 to 17.56% for the flat configuration, 18.16 to 18.62% for the dimpled configuration, and 18.36 to 18.91% for the dimpled–protruded configuration. Compared with the flat configuration, the dimpled–protruded configuration achieves a maximum improvement of 7.63% in exergy efficiency and a 1.60% reduction in entropy generation, while the thermo-exergetic performance factor reaches a maximum value of 0.323. The ANN model exhibits high prediction accuracy and excellent agreement with the experimental results. These findings demonstrate the potential of surface-modified PV/T absorber plates for enhancing thermodynamic performance and provide useful guidance for the design and optimisation of high-performance solar thermal systems for appli