	abstract = {In the global transition to carbon neutrality, hydrogen serves as a vital carrier for storing intermittent renewable energy such as wind and solar power. Proton exchange membrane fuel cells (PEMFC) are essential devices for reconverting hydrogen into electricity. However, the complex multiphysics coupling of gas, water, electricity, and heat makes output behavior difficult to predict through mechanistic models. Consequently, optimizing stack performance by adjusting auxiliary system parameters remains a significant control challenge at current stage. Based on system experiments, machine learning models, and the Shapley Additive Explanation (SHAP) methodology derived from game theory, this paper systematically explores the effects of operating parameters of the hydrogen supply subsystem on the output performance of high-power fuel cell systems. First, controlled-variable experiments were designed to investigate the voltage response under diverse operating conditions. Subsequently, the experimental data were preprocessed and subjected to feature selection to construct a neural network-based prediction model. Finally, interpretability analysis based on SHAP values was conducted globally at different current densities. The results show that at low current density, operating temperature has a significant negative effect on the output voltage, while ambient water vapor pressure ratio exhibits a strong positive effect. At high current density, anode hydrogen concentration, anode molar flow rate, and anode water vapor concentration become the three parameters with the highest contribution. This study quantifies the effects of anode operating parameters on high-power fuel cell system performance, providing optimization solutions for hydrogen supply subsystem and guidance for formulating control strategies to enhance hydrogen utilization efficiency. © 2026 Published by Elsevier Ltd.},
