	affiliations = {Computer and Information Technology Department, The Applied College, King Abdulaziz University, Jeddah, Saudi Arabia},
	abstract = {Artificial Intelligence (AI) facilitates adaptive, real-time energy management in residential microgrids operating under uncertain and dynamic conditions. Traditional rule-based and static optimisation methods are often inadequate for addressing fluctuations in electricity prices, household demand, renewable generation, and electric vehicle (EV) availability. To address these challenges, this study presents a hierarchical real-time energy management framework that integrates Model Predictive Control (MPC) with Deep Double Q-Network (DDQN) reinforcement learning. The proposed framework accommodates microgrids with photovoltaic (PV) generation, hybrid energy storage, and bidirectional Vehicle-to-Home (V2H) and Home-to-Vehicle (H2V) energy exchange. The predictive scheduling layer generates short-term operating plans, while the reinforcement learning layer refines control actions in real time at device, household, and supervisory levels. Simulation results demonstrate that the framework reduces computational burden by 31.4%, grid dependency by 21%, and peak demand by 14–19%, while increasing renewable energy utilisation by 18–25%. The approach also achieves approximately 96% convergence stability and a real-time response time below 0.42 s. These findings indicate that the proposed AI-based framework delivers efficient, robust, and practical real-time coordination for residential microgrids, even in the presence of renewable intermittency and uncertain EV availability. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.},
