	abstract = {Assessing the environmental impact of photovoltaic (PV) systems requires life cycle assessment (LCA). However, conventional LCA is constrained by data scarcity, uncertainty, and high computational demands. Although machine learning (ML) is increasingly applied to PV LCA, its effectiveness remains unclear. This study systematically evaluates the role of ML in enhancing PV LCA by identifying current applications, methodological trends, limitations, and research gaps. A systematic literature review was conducted following the PRISMA guidelines, resulting in the analysis of 32 studies that integrate ML with LCA frameworks for PV systems. The results show that ML applications primarily focus on carbon emissions, which account for 72% of the reviewed studies, while other important environmental impacts, such as water use, toxicity, and land use, remain largely underrepresented. Artificial neural networks dominate the methodological landscape (15 studies), whereas deep learning appears in only seven studies and is primarily used for time series forecasting and image based geospatial analysis. Most models rely on simulation-generated data (13 studies), whereas only eight studies use real world datasets. The review demonstrates that ML has the potential to enhance PV LCA by improving predictive capabilities, reducing computation time, and supporting better decision making. However, challenges related to data quality, model generalizability, and the limited integration of comprehensive LCA indicators persist. Addressing these challenges is essential for advancing the application of ML in PV LCA. © 2026 The Authors. Published by Elsevier Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY license. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/},
	author_keywords = {Life cycle assessment; Machine learning; Photovoltaic; Sustainability},
