	abstract = {This study presents a geometry-aware framework for residential solar energy estimation that integrates unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV)-based photogrammetry, three-dimensional Gaussian Splatting (3DGS), physics-based energy modeling, and multimodal machine learning (ML) for active and passive solar prediction. Building geometries are reconstructed from low-cost UAV video using segment anything model 3 (SAM3) segmentation, structure-from-motion, and dense reconstruction, with 3DGS generating scene representations that preserve roof and facade features relevant to solar analysis, converted into watertight level of detail 3 (LOD3) building envelopes with integrated fenestration and transformed into EnergyPlus-compatible energy models at five performance levels. Validation across 33 residential buildings confirms good agreement between reconstructed and ground-truth dimensions and realistic thermal energy demand trends. Solar simulations evaluate annual internal solar gains, photovoltaic (PV) potential, and sunlit area.A multimodal graph neural network (GNN) based surrogate model uses geometry-aware and scalar building features for rapid, simulation-free estimation, achieving testing R<sup>2</sup> values of 0.890, 0.753, and 0.815 for annual internal solar gain, PV generation, and total sunlit area, respectively. The results demonstrate the potential of geometry-aware scene representations and ML as a scalable approach for rapid solar-performance assessment. © 2026 The Authors. Published by Elsevier Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC license. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/},
