	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035900400?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Department of Industrial Engineering, American Univesity of Sharjah, Sharjah, United Arab Emirates; Department of Mechanical and Industrial Engineering, Liwa University, Abu Dhabi, United Arab Emirates; Faculty of Energy Engineering, Aswan University, Aswan, Egypt; Department of Industrial Engineering and Engineering Management, College of Engineering, University of Sharjah, Sharjah, United Arab Emirates; Benha Faculty of Engineering, Benha University, Benha, Egypt; School of Automation Engineering, University of Electronic Science and Technology of China, Chengdu, China; Institute for Ecology, Technische Univerität Berlin, Berlin, Germany},
	abstract = {Accurate short-term solar irradiance prediction tools are essential in regions with limited ground-based observations to support building-energy applications. Accurate solar irradiance prediction is essential for simulation-based building energy design and operational planning in solar-energy applications, particularly in regions with limited ground-based observations. This study proposes a Double Stacking Ensemble Learning (DSEL) framework that combines diverse base learners, a secondary stack of multiple meta–models, and a Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) strategy to optimize the final weighted aggregation. The framework is trained and evaluated using satellite-derived meteorological, temporal, and geographical predictors from multiple locations across Egypt. Results from site-based cross–validation show that DSEL achieves the best overall performance among the evaluated approaches, with consistently high R <sup>2</sup> and reduced RMSE relative to individual learners and conventional stacking. External validation on geographically unseen sites confirms strong transferability, with tight agreement between predicted and reference irradiance. Robustness tests under noisy and extreme-condition settings further demonstrate that the final ensemble maintains improved performance when prediction becomes more challenging. A consensus feature-importance analysis indicates that the all-sky insolation clearness index is the dominant predictor, with additional contributions from diurnal timing and key meteorological variables, providing a physically consistent interpretation of model behavior. Representative-day case studies show that DSEL reproduces realistic diurnal irradiance cycles across seasons and weather conditions. Overall, the proposed DSEL architecture provides a robust and transferable data-driven tool for solar irradiance prediction in data-scarce environments and supports simulation, planning, and optimization tasks in solar energy engineering. © 2