	abstract = {High penetration of photovoltaic (PV) generation reduces effective system inertia and can lead to critical frequency deviations following disturbances. Although PV deloading provides a cost-effective means of reserving active power without additional hardware, determining the optimal deloaded reserve under weather-driven intermittency remains a key challenge. This study proposes a data-driven framework to determine the optimal PV deloading percentage that satisfies predefined frequency stability constraints while accounting for variability in available PV power. A Random Forest Regression model is developed to predict the frequency nadir of a PV-integrated grid for different operating conditions and deloaded reserve levels. The trained model is subsequently integrated with a Gradient Descent optimizer to identify the optimal deloading percentage that minimizes the deviation between the observed and the acceptable frequency nadir. To construct the dataset, 14,443 dynamic simulation records are generated from a modified IEEE 39-bus test system using DIgSILENT PowerFactory. The model achieves accurate nadir prediction with RMSE values of 0.00094 and 0.0016 for training and test datasets, respectively. Optimization results indicate that the required deloaded reserve increases as available PV generation decreases. Comparative assessment further demonstrates that the optimized deloading strategy provides improved frequency support relative to BESS. Also, for loading variation within the same locality, the resultant optimal deloading percentage adheres to the minimum frequency constraint. Thereby, the proposed approach offers a practical decision-support tool for grid operators to determine optimal PV deloading levels across varying generation scenarios. © 2026 The Authors.},
