	abstract = {Hydrogen is recognized as a promising clean energy carrier and energy storage medium for sustainable energy systems; however, most hydrogen production currently depends on fossil fuels, creating a need for renewable-energy-based alternatives. The purpose of this study is to develop a real-data-driven machine learning framework for solar-based hydrogen production using operational data from a real 10 MW photovoltaic power plant integrated with a proton exchange membrane (PEM) electrolyzer model. The purpose of this study is also to predict next-day temperature, global horizontal irradiance (GHI), power generation, and power plant operational quality using multiple machine learning algorithms. The novelty and originality of this work lie in integrating real operational data from an actual solar power plant with both regression- and classification-based machine learning models coupled with a physics-based PEM electrolyzer model, while also incorporating maintenance-related operational conditions. Unlike previous studies that mainly rely on simulated or meteorological datasets, the proposed framework provides a more realistic and practical approach for forecasting renewable-based hydrogen production. The result of this study demonstrates that the Support Vector Machine (SVM) model achieved the best prediction performance for temperature forecasting with an R² value of 0.94 and for GHI forecasting with an R² value of 0.77, while the Random Forest (RF) model achieved the best performance for power output prediction with an R² value of 0.66. The result of this study also shows that the RF classification model accurately classified the operational quality of the power plant. Furthermore, the estimated annual hydrogen production reached 714.4 kg when 22 kW of electrical power was supplied to the PEM electrolyzer for 6 h per day over 340 operating days annually. Overall, the proposed framework provides a reliable and practical tool for forecasting solar power gen