	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105050386157?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Dept. of Electrical and Electronic Engineering, American International University-Bangladesh, Dhaka, Bangladesh; Dept. of Electrical and Electronic Engineering, Bangladesh University of Engineering and Technology, Dhaka, Bangladesh; Research Centre for Green Energy, Electro-Anatomy, and Digital Intelligence (ReGED), Dhaka, Bangladesh},
	abstract = {The increasing penetration of inverter-based renewable energy resources introduces complex voltage, frequency, and power-dynamic interactions that challenge conventional smart-grid stability assessment methods. This study proposes a Physics-Informed Graph-Attention Feature Fusion Network (PI-GAFFN) for topology-aware, interpretable, and multi-task smart-grid stability assessment. The investigation is organized into two complementary tracks. First, a detailed renewable-integrated electrical system comprising a 100 MWp photovoltaic plant, a 60 MWh battery energy storage system, a virtual-synchronous-machine-based grid-forming converter, medium-voltage collection networks, and a 230 kV grid interface is developed in MATLAB/Simulink–Simscape Electrical. Its dynamic behavior is examined under renewable-power variation, load switching, grid outage, temporary three-phase fault, and reactive-power-support conditions. Second, the UCI Electrical Grid Stability Simulated Data benchmark is transformed into a four-node physics-consistent graph and used independently for PI-GAFFN training, validation, and testing. The proposed network integrates physics-aware node encoding, physics-guided graph attention, feature fusion, and a multi-task objective for simultaneous continuous stability-index regression and stable/unstable classification. PI-GAFFN achieves 99.50% accuracy, 99.48% precision, 99.74% recall, 99.61% F1-score, and 99.98% ROC–AUC, outperforming LR, SVM, RF, XGBoost, CNN, BiLSTM, and CNN–BiLSTM under identical test conditions. For continuous stability estimation, the clean-data evaluation yields an RMSE of 0.012 and an (R<sup>2</sup>) of 0.9987. Robustness tests retain 93.00% accuracy under 10% Gaussian noise and 92.80% under 10% false-data injection, while the model requires approximately 0.07 ms per sample for inference. Attention and ablation analyses further demonstrate the contribution of topology-aware interactions and physics-informed feature learning. The results indicate that PI-GAFFN provides a promising physics-guided framework for accurate, robust, and interpretable stability assessment of renewable-integrated smart grids. © 2026 The Authors.},
