	abstract = {Accelerating the design of high-efficiency perovskite and emerging thin-film solar cells requires systematic exploration of a vast, multidimensional parameter space simultaneously involving absorber, electron transport layer (ETL), hole transport layer (HTL), and window layer materials. We present a physics-informed machine learning (PIML) framework incorporating 6168 validated SCAPS-1D simulations drawn from 10,000 Latin Hypercube-sampled configurations covering 7835 unique four-layer device architectures across 82 distinct absorbers — the broadest absorber coverage of any SCAPS-ML study to date (studies reporting larger simulation counts, e.g., 35,585, are restricted to a single absorber family). Among six regression algorithms tested on identical train/test splits, XGBoost provides the best balance of accuracy, efficiency, and interpretability (R<sup>2</sup> = 0.839, MAE = 1.96%, RMSE = 3.22%), with 3.6× faster training than Gradient Boosting and native compatibility with exact SHAP TreeExplainer. The model employs 55 physics-derived features — including band offsets, carrier lifetimes, diffusion lengths, depletion widths, and absorption fractions — achieving an external validation R<sup>2</sup> = 0.969 on a completely withheld absorber family (CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>3</sub>SnI<sub>3</sub>, n = 320). SHAP analysis reveals that the valence-band offset between absorber and HTL (VBO<sub>abs</sub>/HTL) is the dominant predictor of PCE — surpassing even the absorber bandgap — a cross-material finding with direct implications for lead-free HTL selection, confirmed across three independent validation stages. Leave-one-material-out (LOMO) cross-validation across all 81 absorber families yields a median holdout R<sup>2</sup> = 0.65, confirming broad cross-material generalisation within the explored chemical space. Bootstrap uncertainty quantification (100 resamples) confirms model stability (R<sup>2</sup> = 0.840 ± 0.008) and provides 90% prediction intervals (mean width 3.1 pp) for all top-ranked candidates. Dedicated SCAPS-1D parametric sweeps confirm 19 of 20 ML predictions within ±1% PCE (MAE = 0.30 ± 0.24%), identify 1000 nm as the optimal universal absorber thickness, and establish a CBO window of 0–0.3 eV with a critical defect threshold at N<sub>t</sub>∼10<sup>13</sup>cm<sup>−3</sup>. Top-ranked lead-free configurations from ML — CsSnCl<sub>3</sub>/TiO<sub>2</sub>/CBTS (33.2%), Cs<sub>2</sub>AgInBr<sub>5</sub>Cl/SnO<sub>2</sub>/CBTS (32.98%), and BaZrSe<sub>3</sub>/TiO<sub>2</sub>/MoO<sub>3</sub> (32.95%) all values are simulation upper bounds, with experimental efficiencies estimated 8–15 percentage points lower due to SCAPS idealizations) — have no experimental device yet reported, making them direct fabrication targets. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.},
