	abstract = {Solar–hydrogen hybrid systems can provide low-carbon, dispatchable power, yet many studies assume freshwater availability and rely on static dispatch strategies that are sensitive to climatic variability. This work develops a closed-loop Solar–Green Hydrogen Hybrid System (SGHHS. A five-layer supervisory architecture is implemented in which a Random Forest model predicts a physics-informed PV availability index from meteorological data and supports forecast-informed, rule-based dispatch under component and storage constraints. The system (22.75 MW PV, 2.25 MW electrolyzer, 450 kg H₂ storage, 1 MW fuel cell) is evaluated using long-horizon, hourly simulations under a consistent day/night demand profile. Results indicate an LCOE of USD 0.19–0.23 /kWh, corresponding to an 8–12% reduction relative to a static baseline, with smaller but consistent gains observed under a real-year test. The proposed controller improves dispatch tracking by approximately 22% and reduces hydrogen overproduction and curtailment by about 20%. Overall, the findings show that ML-enabled availability forecasting combined with closed-loop water–energy integration can enhance the operational robustness and sustainability of SGHHS. © 2026 The Authors.},
