	affiliations = {Department of Research and Innovation, Saveetha School of Engineering, SIMATS, Tamil Nadu, Chennai, 602105, India},
	abstract = {Photovoltaic (PV) systems operate under highly dynamic environmental conditions, where irradiance variability, temperature fluctuations, and progressive degradation induce severe domain shifts that degrade the reliability of conventional data-driven fault diagnosis models. Existing deep learning and domain adaptation approaches largely assume stationary data distributions, lack physical consistency, and rarely quantify diagnostic uncertainty, leading to false alarms and poor generalization in real-world deployments. To address these limitations, this study proposes PhyDeOT-Net, a Physics-Guided Degradation-Aware Optimal Transport Network for robust cross-domain PV fault diagnosis. The proposed framework integrates a physics-guided spectral operator for multi-scale electrical feature extraction, a degradation–fault disentanglement module to isolate aging effects from structural faults, a physics-conditioned progressive optimal transport alignment strategy to mitigate degradation-induced distribution drift, and a hyperbolic evidential classification layer for hierarchical fault modeling and uncertainty quantification. The model is implemented using Python and PyTorch, with Fast Fourier Transform-based spectral learning and Sinkhorn-based optimal transport alignment. Experiments are conducted on a real grid-tied PV dataset comprising electrical and environmental measurements (voltage, current, irradiance, temperature) with derived degradation indicators such as estimated series resistance. Results demonstrate that PhyDeOT-Net achieves 98.62% accuracy and 0.995 AUC, outperforming CNN–LSTM and domain-adaptive baselines by up to 15.37% accuracy improvement and reducing domain gap to 1.3%. The framework maintains stable performance under irradiance, temperature, and degradation domain shifts, while evidential learning provides calibrated uncertainty estimates for reliable decision-making. In conclusion, PhyDeOT-Net offers a physically consistent, degradation-aware, and uncertainty-informed diagnostic solution, enabling reliable and scalable PV monitoring in real-world non-stationary operating environments. © 2026 The Author(s).},
