	abstract = {Direct normal irradiance (DNI) is crucial for generating electricity from concentrated solar power plants. DNI attenuation is minimal in clean, clear skies but varies greatly on cloudy days. Likewise, under clear skies with aerosols dominating, DNI is reduced due to scattering and absorption. This study investigated the impact of aerosols on DNI prediction using a deep learning model in a low-aerosol-loading area. A reliable forecast model for all sky conditions was developed using Long Short-Term Memory. The model used two sources of aerosol optical depth (AOD) data: one from the Aerosol Robotic Network (AERONET) stations and the other from the Himawari-8/9 satellites. Overall, performance improvements are mainly observed under clear-sky and high-AOD conditions, whereas gains are limited under cloudy or partly cloudy conditions, particularly when satellite-derived AOD is used. On clear days, RMSE and MAE are reduced by 4.35% and 5.93%, respectively, with AERONET AOD input, and by 2.42% and 4.85%, respectively, with Himawari 8/9 input. The results, using an explainable AI (XAI) technique, show that AOD plays a more significant role on high AOD loading days. As expected, cloud cover is the dominant contributor to reduced forecast performance, and adding AOD during cloudy periods does not yield the same improvement as during clear-sky periods. © 2026 The Author(s).},
