	abstract = {Accurate forecasting of photovoltaic (PV) power generation is critical for grid stability, energy trading, and capacity planning due to the strong temporal variability and spatial diversity of solar resources. This study evaluates short-term PV power forecasting across a three-level spatial hierarchy in Belgium, including national, regional, and provincial series from January 2020 to March 2025. A diverse set of statistical, machine learning, foundation, and hybrid deep learning models was assessed for 24-hour-ahead forecasting using meteorological, calendar, and periodic variables as exogenous inputs. Six cross-sectional reconciliation methods were assessed: Bottom-Up, Ordinary Least Squares, Weighted Least Squares, Structural Scaling, Covariance-based, and Shrinkage-based. Among all reconciled configurations, XGBoost with covariance-based estimator and LightGBM with Bottom-Up achieved the lowest NRMSE, both reaching 0.74. Reconciliation was associated with larger relative reductions from the Bottom-Up approach in the deep learning models, with Weighted Least Squares reducing NRMSE by up to 50.8% for KAN (0.93), 39.5% for NBEATSx (0.96), and 30.7% for NHITS (1.24). TBATS with Bottom-Up achieved an NRMSE of 1.02, and TimeGPT with the covariance-based estimator reached 1.08, with reconciliation yielding only a small reduction of around 2%. Taken together, these findings suggest that, in this study, machine learning models tended to achieve the best forecasting accuracy, while reconciliation contributed to hierarchical coherence and yielded model-dependent performance changes. © 2026 The Authors.},
