	affiliations = {MACSI, Department of Mathematics and Statistics, University of Limerick, Limerick, Ireland; AirPV, Cork, Ireland; School of Mathematics and Maxwell Institute for Mathematical Science, University of Edinburgh, Edinburgh, United Kingdom},
	abstract = {Accurate solar irradiance estimation is essential for reliable modelling of solar photovoltaic power production. In Ireland’s mid-to-high latitude maritime climate, solar estimation is shaped by seasonal variation, low winter sun angles, frequent cloud variability, sparse ground-based measurements and discrepancies among satellite products, making solar irradiance dataset selection challenging. We develop a Bayesian spatio-temporal framework for country-specific irradiance estimates across Ireland using openly available data. The model produces hourly and sub-hourly irradiance estimates with full uncertainty quantification, and is designed to support site evaluation and near-real-time solar applications. Cross-validation demonstrates robust performance across temporal resolutions, with the 30-minute model achieving highest precision, while the 10-minute model provides improved uncertainty quantification. We compare model outputs with ERA5 reanalysis data, nearest-station interpolation, and satellite-based data from Copernicus Atmosphere Monitoring Service (CAMS). Our Irish model outperforms ERA5 at hourly resolutions, reducing Root Mean Square Error from 82 to 36 W m<sup>−2</sup>, highlighting the limitations of coarse-resolution global reanalysis products in Ireland’s cloudy climate. We also present the first evaluation of Irish irradiance data from CAMS. For hourly irradiance, CAMS outperform ERA5 and our model further reduces RMSE (40 to 35 W m<sup>−2</sup>). For photovoltaic power estimation, our framework remains competitive and provides uncertainty quantification that is unavailable for satellite and reanalysis datasets. Our framework’s value is demonstrated through comparisons with photovoltaic power output from residential, industrial and utility sites in Ireland, showing agreement with observed generation and providing an open-source irradiance dataset with uncertainty for high resolution resource assessment and improved estimation of system losses (e.g.clipping). © 2026 The Authors. Published by Elsevier Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY license. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/},
