	abstract = {The de-rating control of photovoltaic (PV) systems where an amount of power is reserved for the ancillary services is becoming popular amongst the researchers. In this control, the optimal approach for the amount of reserve is to keep it as a fixed percentage of the maximum power. Thus, during the operational dynamics in PVs, the assessment of maximum power is crucial, few existing works have utilized additional hardware and couple of sensors for the assessment, the high cost of these devices is a setback. In this work, yearly irradiance and temperature data of Chandigarh, Union Territory (U.T.), India has been collected to train machine learning regression models, the models predict the maximum power under dynamic operations. The collected data is processed before it is fed to the learning models, the adopted training strategy eliminates the requirement of any other hardware for the predictions. Reference power tracking algorithm is also developed in this work, the algorithm provides the flexibility for operation on either side on the power–voltage curve of the PVs. The performance of the best model is compared with the standard maximum power point tracking algorithm and the de-rating operation is verified on the DC system as well as on the AC microgrid. The simulations are carried out in the SIMULINK environment of MATLAB 2024a. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved. This article is available under the terms of the https://publishingsupport.iopscience.iop.org/iop-standard/v1.},
