	abstract = {We present a plantwide dynamic optimization framework for optimal economic operation of a multi-concentrator hybrid solar thermal power plant (HSTPP) integrating parabolic trough collectors (PTC) and a linear Fresnel reflector (LFR). The existing first-principles HSTPP model involves more than 120,000 coupled ordinary differential equations obtained after spatial discretization of distributed parameter concentrator models. While it provides detailed dynamic representation of interactions across multiple concentrators, heat-exchangers, and the power block, it remains computationally prohibitive for use in plantwide optimization. To enable tractable plantwide dynamic optimization, neural network (NN) based digital-twin framework is developed by replacing the concentrator models with data-driven surrogates. A feedforward NN predicts PTC outlet oil temperature, while a nonlinear finite impulse response long short-term memory model with moving window average captures steam drum pressure of the LFR-Steam drum (SD) subsystem. Both surrogates are trained using dynamic data from a validated first-principles simulator and achieve high predictive accuracy in infinite-step-ahead simulation, with the PTC-FFNN model giving (Formula presented), RMSE 2.58 °C, and fit 93.96%, and the LFR-SD NFIR-LSTM model giving (Formula presented), RMSE 1.12 bar, and fit 91.19%. The resulting gray-box HSTPP-Surrogate model reduces the standalone plantwide simulation time from 42.7 min to 1.33 min, corresponding to a 32.1 times speed-up. The surrogate-based multi-concentrator digital twin is integrated with a first-principles model of balance-of-plant components of the HSTPP to solve a market-driven revenue maximization problem using a genetic algorithm. The resulting optimal operation increases daily revenue from Rs. 26,424 to Rs. 37,199, corresponding to a 40.78% improvement over the nominal fixed-flow base operation. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for 