	abstract = {To fully realize the clean energy potential of flexible, lightweight, and low-cost organic solar cells (OSCs), material discovery must shift from slow and inefficient trial-and-error to novel accelerated and machine learning based methods. Currently, optimization restarts with each new material, and progress could be significantly accelerated by obtaining comparable, material-independent and readily measurable predictive proxies. Aiming to identify such transferable properties linking molecular structure to macroscopic device performance, we built on the frameworks of Kasha and Spano to extract robust, physically meaningful microstructural proxies directly from UV-vis spectra via the quantitative analysis of vibronic structures. We generated a large dataset pairing five state-of-the-art polymer donors (D) and five non-fullerene acceptors (A) in a combinatorial grid with a wide range of process conditions tested for solar cells of each donor–acceptor (D:A) pair. We then developed a generalized spectral deconvolution routine to extract reliable optical features. Leveraging these features, we applied Gaussian process regression (GPR) to predict current–voltage characteristics. Crucially, the model successfully extrapolated to untrained, novel material combinations, achieving a mean absolute percentage error (MAPE) below 10%. Additionally, all solar cells were degraded under accelerated conditions, and the short circuit current losses could be directly correlated to aggregation features of the acceptor. This work provides significant progress toward the creation of digital twins for OPV, leading to the possibility of inverse molecular design and acceleration in optimizing efficiency and stability. This journal is © The Royal Society of Chemistry, 2026.},
