	abstract = {Integrating solar energy production with agriculture through agrivoltaic (APV) systems substantially alters rainfall distribution, runoff processes, and soil-erosion rates. The Revised Universal Soil Loss Equation (RUSLE) and other empirical soil erosion models were developed for traditional agricultural environments and do not account for the spatial complexities introduced by APV systems. This review evaluates these empirical models in the context of APV farms and highlights their key limitations in accurately capturing changes in rainfall distribution, runoff behavior, and erosion risk. Specifically, solar panels induce an uneven rainfall distribution, leading to concentrated runoff and an increased erosion risk. They also alter the microclimate beneath and between panels, affecting vegetation growth and soil susceptibility to erosion. As a result, empirical soil erosion models such as RUSLE require substantial modifications to predict soil loss accurately. Critical parameters—rainfall erosivity (R), soil erodibility (K), slope length and steepness (LS), cover management (C), and support practices (P)—require systematic adaptation to APV-specific conditions. We identify critical knowledge gaps in applying these models to APV environments and underscore the imperative for high-resolution monitoring of soil moisture, runoff, and erosion rates. We further highlight the need to develop machine-learning-assisted models to improve soil erosion predictions under APV conditions. Such integrated modeling approaches will improve the capacity to balance renewable energy production with agricultural soil conservation. © 2025 The Author(s). Published by Elsevier B.V. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/},
