	affiliations = {Faculty of Electrical Engineering, Universiti Teknologi MARA, Selangor, Shah Alam, Malaysia; Solar Research Institute (SRI), Universiti Teknologi MARA, Selangor, Shah Alam, Malaysia},
	abstract = {Photovoltaic (PV) systems have emerged as a pivotal solution in the transition to renewable energy, offering sustainable power generation with minimal environmental impact. However, their efficiency is hindered by factors such as weather variability, equipment degradation, and operational anomalies. Predicting PV performance accurately is essential for optimizing energy output and ensuring system reliability. This study presents a hybrid Artificial Neural Network (ANN) model that enhanced the ANN with Single Candidate Optimizer (SCO) to predict PV output, focusing on two key metrics: power daily max and total daily energy. Using real data from a Large Scale Solar Park with an AC capacity of 50 MW, the hybrid model was developed and evaluated against hybrid Evolutionary Programming (EP–ANN) and ANN. Results demonstrate that total daily energy predictions yield higher accuracy and reliability, with R values exceeding 0.96 as compared to the prediction of daily power maximum. For both case studies, the hybrid SCO–ANN model consistently outperformed other techniques, achieving superior optimization and predictive accuracy. This research underscores the potential of hybrid models in advancing PV performance prediction, paving the way for more efficient renewable energy solutions. © 2026, IPMEDIA SDN BHD. All rights reserved.},
