	abstract = {Soiling on photovoltaic (PV) modules, caused by dust and pollutants, reduces solar energy output by limiting light transmission and efficiency. This study aims to predict soiling-induced energy loss and develop data-driven models incorporating key environmental variables influencing PV performance. Firstly, multiple predictors, including solar irradiance and rainfall, were evaluated for their impact on energy production and soiling losses. However, rainfall was ultimately excluded from the final model due to poor temporal alignment with soiling events and insufficient correlation at the operational timescale considered. Then, machine learning models, including linear regression, decision trees, and random forest, were employed to capture the relationship between irradiance-driven soiling effects and energy loss. The optimal model achieved a high predictive accuracy, with an R-squared value of 0.9894, a mean absolute error (MAE) of 0.0009, and a root mean squared error (RMSE) of 0.0296, representing a 15–30% improvement over conventional machine-learning approaches. These results demonstrate the capability to capture nonlinear soiling–irradiance interactions despite the exclusion of rainfall as a direct predictor. This study presents an accurate predictive framework for forecasting soiling-related energy losses with practical insights for PV system operators to improve maintenance and cleaning schedules for long-term energy efficiency. © 2026},
