	abstract = {This study aims to explore a photovoltaic power prediction model based on deep learning to improve the reliability and economic benefits of photovoltaic power generation systems. The study first reviews the current status and challenges of photovoltaic power prediction and analyzes the limitations of traditional prediction methods. Subsequently, a hybrid deep learning model combining long short-term memory network (LSTM) and convolutional neural network (CNN) is proposed. This model fully utilizes the advantages of LSTM in time series data processing and the ability of CNN in feature extraction to improve prediction accuracy. The study uses historical power generation data and meteorological data of a photovoltaic power station as input, and constructs a prediction model through data preprocessing, feature engineering and model training. Experimental results show that the proposed hybrid deep learning model is superior to traditional statistical methods and single deep learning models in short-term and medium-term photovoltaic power prediction, and the average prediction error is reduced by 15%. In addition, the study also explores the impact of different meteorological factors on prediction accuracy and proposes a model optimization strategy. The results of this study provide a new method for the accurate prediction of photovoltaic power, which is of great significance to improving the scheduling efficiency and economic benefits of photovoltaic power generation systems. © 2026 The Authors.},
