	abstract = {Climate change is intensifying extreme temperature events, especially heatwaves, which affect both energy demand and the performance of photovoltaic systems in the metropolitan region of São Paulo, particularly in the municipality of Santo André. The central problem lies in the difficulty of accurately estimating solar generation during these extreme events, as photovoltaic panels exhibit complex behavior. Although heatwaves are associated with higher solar radiation due to reduced cloud cover, high temperatures significantly reduce module efficiency, potentially causing losses of up to half a percent for each degree Celsius above standard conditions. This study presents a machine-learning-based methodology using the Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) algorithm to estimate photovoltaic generation while explicitly considering the effects of heatwaves and future climate projections. Using historical data from the Brazilian Daily Weather Gridded Data dataset, combined with extreme temperature event indices calculated with Climpact software and projections from four global climate models under the SSP2-4.5 and SSP5-8.5 scenarios, the developed model estimated photovoltaic energy generation. During the validation, the XGBoost model achieved a coefficient of determination greater than 0.996. The distinguishing contribution lies in the explicit integration of heatwave indices as predictive variables and systematic analysis of future climate scenarios up to 2100. Results from the ensemble of four climate models indicate that annual mean generation decreases by 0.33% under SSP2-4.5, and 0.37% under SSP5-8.5 over 2021-2100, with monthly reductions reaching up to 3.92% under the high-emission scenario, providing quantitative tools for energy planning in systems with high solar radiation incidence.  © 2026 The Authors.},
