	affiliations = {School of Information Engineering, Yellow River Conservancy Technical University, Kaifeng, 475004, China; Faculty of Information Technology, Nguyen Tat Thanh University, Ho Chi Minh City, Viet Nam},
	abstract = {AbstractThis study investigates the effect of phase change materials (PCM) and nanofluids in the photovoltaic-thermal (PVT) systems. Seven configurations were experimentally evaluated, PVT, PVT-Water, PVT-TiO<sub>2</sub>, PVT-ZnO, PVT-PCM-Water, PVT-PCM-TiO<sub>2</sub>, and PVT-PCM-ZnO. Performance was characterized through energy and exergy analysis, while machine learning models (XGBoost, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting) were trained on the experimental dataset to predict system performance and identify dominant influencing factors. A 40 Wp monocrystalline silicon PV module with copper sheet-and-tube thermal collector was used. Nanofluids were prepared at 0.5% volume fraction using TiO<sub>2</sub> and ZnO. Both nanoparticles dispersed in distilled water via two-step ultrasonication. Paraffin wax with the latent heat of 200 kJ/kg served as thermal storage medium. Experiments were conducted under clear-sky conditions in Qinghai Province, China. The measurements were recorded at 30-min intervals from 10:30 to 17:00. Hydrogen production was predicted using Faraday's law assuming a 20-cell PEM electrolyzer functions at 38 V with 0.99 Faradaic efficiency. For the hydrogen production calculation PVT electrical output was used. Performance metrics including electrical, thermal, overall, and exergy efficiencies were evaluated alongside predicted hydrogen production rates. PVT-PCM-TiO<sub>2</sub> achieved maximum electrical efficiency of 19.02% compared to 11.46% of conventional PVT. Overall efficiency reached 47.13% at 15:00, while exergy efficiency peaked at 17.83% at 17:00. Predicted hydrogen production attained 99.2 mL/min for PVT-PCM-TiO<sub>2</sub>/NF at 12:30. PCM-integrated systems reported superior late-afternoon performance by recording the hydrogen production rates above 24 mL/min at 17:00 compared to below 17 mL/min for non-PCM cases. Machine learning analysis using XGBoost, Random Forest, and Gradient Boosting Regressor achieved R2 values exceeding 0.96 across all essential parameters. Further the feature importance analysis identified time of day and PCM integration (0.240) as dominant predictive factors with the value of 0.470 and 0.240. All things considered the current work establish PVT-PCM-nanofluid systems as effective solutions for high-efficiency renewable hydrogen production in arid regions. © 2026 Hydrogen Energy Publications LLC. Published by Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
