	affiliations = {College of Computing and Information Sciences, University of Technology and Applied Sciences, Musandam, Oman; College of Engineering and Technology, University of Technology and Applied Sciences, Musandam, Oman; Department of Computer Science and Engineering, GMR Institute of Technology, Andhra Pradesh, Rajam, India},
	abstract = {The integration of solar and wind energy systems for green hydrogen production has emerged as a promising solution for sustainable energy generation. This study investigates the application of various deep learning models for the regression-based prediction of key performance metric levelized cost of hydrogen (LCOH), levelized cost of electricity (LCOE), and excess energy—using a comprehensive experimental dataset collected from HOMER PRO, a trustworthy optimization tool. The dataset comprises 18,000 instances with 35 features, including 32 independent variables and 3 dependent targets: LCOH, LCOE, and excess energy. To address complex, nonlinear relationships and potential multicollinearity among features, we evaluated the performance of several neural network architectures: Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN), Recurrent Neural Networks (RNN), Gated Recurrent Units (GRU), Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP), Transformer encoders, and Autoencoder-based models. All six models were trained using the Adam optimizer, with mean squared error as the loss function and mean absolute error as the evaluation metric. Mean Absolute Error (MAE), Root Mean Square Error (RMSE), and Coefficient of Determination (R<sup>2</sup>-score) are used to test the reliability of the 6 different deep learning models on 18000 sets of data that had different combinations of system capacities and identified features. Among all models, the Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) consistently achieved the lowest error metrics and the highest R<sup>2</sup> scores across all three outputs. For LCOH, the MLP yielded an MAE of 0.00385 and an R<sup>2</sup> score of 0.9998, indicating highly accurate predictions. Similarly, for LCOE and Excess Energy, the MLP achieved MAE values of 0.00010 and 0.2035, with R<sup>2</sup>-scores of 0.9997 and 0.9986, respectively. These results suggest that the MLP is particularly well-suited for this tabular regression task, likely due to its capacity to model both linear and 