	affiliations = {School of Materials Science and Engineering, Pusan National University, Busan, 46241, South Korea; Research Institute for Energy Efficient Technologies, National Institute of Advanced Industrial Science and Technology (AIST), Ibaraki, Tsukuba, 305-8568, Japan},
	abstract = {Ge-incorporated kesterite (Cu₂Zn(Sn₁₋ₓGeₓ)Se₄, CZTGSe) is considered an alternative to Cu(In,Ga)Se₂; however, its conversion efficiency remains limited by significant open-circuit voltage ( V <sub>OC</sub>) losses. One possible reason for this loss is the presence of internal defects in the kesterite, which are closely related to the composition ratio of its thin film. In this study, a machine learning (ML)-based analysis is performed to investigate the effects of elemental composition ratios in kesterite devices. A comprehensive database is compiled by integrating composition analysis results with device parameters obtained from over 6000 self-fabricated CZTGSe thin films and their devices. Particularly, the composition ratio is distinguished between the surface and bulk to clarify their respective roles in governing device performance. To ensure reliable analysis, various ML models, including Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, LightGBM, and XGBoost, are employed to evaluate feature importance and demonstrated consistent predictive capability. The results reveal that the Ge/IV (IV = Sn+Ge) ratio at the surface is the most influential feature, decisively affecting the overall conversion efficiency, V <sub>OC</sub>, and short-circuit current. Its dominant role is associated with forming an adaptable conduction-band offset and suppressing Sn-related defect states at the buffer/CZTGSe interface. These results indicate that surface engineering, especially Ge/IV, is a key factor in improving the performance of kesterite solar cells. © 2025 The Authors.},
