	abstract = {Solar power is environmentally friendly and therefore plays an important role in the global transition toward clean production of energy. One of the major problems is that the output of PV systems changes in time, depending on weather conditions and solar irradiance. Thus, it is difficult for them to be an adequate and reliable source of the electricity. To address this problem, we developed a prediction algorithm for forecasting solar power generation up to one day ahead. To estimate electricity generation, we first used a simple physical formula as a baseline and then applied statistical analysis and K-means clustering to identify historical patterns. Next, we developed a dedicated hybrid CNN-LSTM neural network. The model was evaluated on day-ahead forecasting tasks and achieved an average absolute error of approximately 0.9095. We also applied ensemble boosting to weather features, which improved wind speed prediction accuracy by approximately two thirds. These findings demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed prediction framework. The proposed methodology improves the accuracy of solar power generation forecasting. It can support better power system management and improve the utilization of renewable energy. © 2026 The Authors. Published by Elsevier B.V.},
