	abstract = {Accurate photovoltaic cell modeling is essential for reliable performance prediction, yet parameter estimation remains challenging for widely used electrical equivalent models. This work investigates four PV cell models: (i) ideal, (ii) single-diode model (SDM), (iii) double-diode model (DDM), and (iv) three-diode model (TDM). Each one undergoes a rigorous investigation of its intrinsic, unobservable parameters employing two distinct methodologies: (i) the identification approach and (ii) the adjustment technique. Several numerical and metaheuristic techniques are employed, including MATLAB nonlinear solvers, Newton–Raphson, genetic algorithms (GAs), and the gradient evolution (GE) method. Furthermore, an artificial neural network (ANN)–based identification scheme also serves to improve the quality of such hybrid solutions. A comparative analysis of the four models is conducted and validated through theoretical and experimental results. The findings show that the ideal electrical model combined with ANN provides the best performance, achieving a very low maximum deviation (ξ<sub>r</sub> = 2.1 × 10<sup>−9</sup>) and a high fill factor of 78.97%, demonstrating its effectiveness for accurate PV parameter estimation and PV behavior prediction. Copyright © 2026 Rabeb Abid et al. International Transactions on Electrical Energy Systems published by John Wiley & Sons Ltd.},
