	abstract = {Integrating solar power into electricity grids is complex because of dynamic environmental conditions. Module temperature significantly affects the efficiency of solar photovoltaic (PV) systems, leading to power output that deviates from theoretical values. Large-scale renewable energy facilities depend heavily on accurate predictions of solar power production. Ongoing research and development are necessary to identify the optimal machine-learning method for forecasting solar PV power generation. Various machine learning (ML) techniques, such as artificial neural networks (ANNs) and random forests, have shown promise in this domain. Two ML models, ML1 (Machine learning model without considering module temperature) and ML2 (Machine learning model considering module temperature), were developed in this study to evaluate the impact of module temperature on power performance. The National Renewable Energy Laboratory is used to obtain data on climatic conditions and solar PV output, with and without accounting for module temperature, for monocrystalline silicon PV modules in Uttarakhand, India. Five ML techniques, such as ANN-Particle swarm optimization (PSO), ANN, Gaussian Process Regression, Decision Tree, and support vector machine algorithms, with and without considering the module temperature, are used in this study. The ANN-PSO model demonstrated exceptional performance across multiple metrics, yielding mean squared error (MSE), root mean square error (RMSE), R², MAPE, and mean absolute error (MAE) of 0.052, 0.2280, 0.9992, 0.9913, 0.0021, and 0.00315, respectively, accounting for module temperature. Even without considering module temperature, the model still achieved notable results with MSE, RMSE, R², MAPE, and MAE values of 0.032, 0.1812, 0.9913, 0.0021, and 0.00315, respectively. These findings underscore the effectiveness of the ANN-PSO approach in accurately predicting under varying conditions. © 2026 The Authors},
