	abstract = {The increasing popularity of Electric Vehicles (EVs) highlights the necessity for reliable and secure charging infrastructure. Abnormal events like overcurrent can threaten battery longevity and safety, leading to damage and inefficiencies. This study employs real-world data from a functional EV charging station, focusing on operational parameters such as voltage, current, temperature, state of charge, and power, to develop a data-driven approach for detecting operational anomalies. A novel framework is introduced that leverages multi-parameter sensor datasets and combines Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) with statistical modeling to characterize unique anomaly signatures. The analysis covers battery state of charge over 370 charging cycles to identify underlying trends and notable deviations through smoothing and curve-fitting techniques. The State-of-Charge (SoC) was approximately from 40% to 63% over the analyzed period, whereas temperature was approximately 15°C to 35°C. Anomaly events were firstly observed by scatter plots of current and voltage with temperature. The anomaly events corresponded to currents exceeding approximately 2.5 A, reaching up to around 3.0 A. The results align with the statistical analysis. The high current status is also strongly associated with the elevated power levels (frequently above 7.5 W, peaking near 9.5 W) and increased temperatures (often above 25°C, reaching up to 35°C). For the validation of results, a machine learning model was trained and from the feature importance score we concluded that current plays a vital role in anomaly occurrence. These findings lay the groundwork for creating reliable anomaly detection systems and facilitating predictive maintenance to boost lifetime and performance. © 2013 IEEE.},
