	abstract = {Estimation of solar photovoltaic (PV) power generation is necessary to predict accurately the further functioning of the grid, energy planning, and massive integration of renewable energy sources. Nevertheless, dynamic nature of PV systems, intermittent solar irradiance, and meteorological conditions which change at a very high rate all render it challenging to have a reliable prediction. This paper proposes a Hybrid Machine Learning Approach of a Dynamic Weighting Mechanism (DWM-HMLA) in the short-term solar PV power prediction. The presented framework combines various heterogeneous machine learning models into an ensemble structure, in which a dynamic weighting scheme dynamically modulates the contribution of individual base learners in real-time according to the current environmental factors and recent errors in prediction. The proposed DWM-HMLA was tested with the help of the real-life solar PV data taken in various geographical areas and under various meteorological conditions. The proposed architecture is a combination of Support Vector Regression (SVR), Random Forest (RF), and Artificial Neural Network (ANN) models in an ensemble architecture. It has been experimentally demonstrated that the proposed method attains a Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) of 0.214 kW, a Mean Absolute Error (MAE) of 0.167 kW, and a coefficient of determination (R<sup>2</sup>) of 0.962. The forecasting accuracy of the proposed approach is 12.8% and 15.3% higher in the average of RMSE and MAE respectively. The adaptive weighting mechanism allows the non-linear time patterns to be processed in an effective manner and also ensures robustness when the weather conditions change rapidly. The findings indicate that DWM-HMLA is a stable and scaled forecasting platform, which can be used to integrate renewable energy, balance grids, and energy market. Proper predictions made by the suggested model can help the operators of the systems and policymakers to make the most out of their resource allocation and minimize reliance on standard power generation. The results of forecasting the proposed DWM-HMLA can be implemented directly in the short-term operational decision making in grid management whereby precision intraday solar PV power estimates are needed to adjust unit commitment to the reserve commitment and real-time load balancing. © 2026, Institute for Environmental Nanotechnology. All rights reserved.},
