	abstract = {In low-voltage distribution areas, especially where agricultural industries account for a large share, production loads exhibit pronounced intra-day peaks and valleys. With large-scale interconnection of distributed generation, the temporal misalignment between load profiles and distributed generation output leads to frequent short-term source–load mismatch. Limited monitoring and communication infrastructure further increases operational complexity, and conventional network reinforcement and hardware upgrades are costly and yield low returns. To address these challenges, this paper proposes a dual-layer adaptive control strategy for coordinated distributed energy storage and reactive power devices is proposed. The upper-layer peak-shaving and valley-filling control strategy formulates a daily active power output plan for energy storage systems based on load and photovoltaic forecasts, which is dynamically adjusted intra-day according to the actual state of charge (SOC), real load data and photovoltaic generation data. The lower-layer active and reactive power voltage control strategy, based on a fully connected neural network and gradient descent, develops a method for coordinated active and reactive power voltage regulation tailored for low-measurement environments. It adaptively controls reactive power compensation devices and energy storage systems when node voltages exceed limits, thus mitigating voltage excursions. Simulation results indicate that this control strategy, which is independent of global measurement data, fully leverages the discrete adjustment capability of reactive power compensation devices and the fine-tuning capability of energy storage systems, effectively addressing source–load imbalances and voltage excursions. © 2026},
