	abstract = {Traditional solar trackers often lose energy and experience mechanical problems in cloudy weather because they react rigidly to changing conditions. Studies evaluated the Hybrid LSTM-DQN control on testing dataset of 120 hours of high-resolution (1-minute) weather data to provide a fair assessment. This paper suggests a new control system to fix these problems. It combines a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) network, which predicts how much sunlight there will be soon, with a Deep Q-Network (DQN) reinforcement learning agent, which makes the best decisions. The LSTM predicts sunlight intensity for the coming 10 minutes with a Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) of 42.8 W/m<sup>2</sup>. This prediction helps the DQN controller. When tested using 120 hours of weather data, the new system had a Net Energy Gain of 17.8% (28.4 kWh) compared to a regular PID tracker. The system also stopped unnecessary movements, cutting down actuator uses by 34.8% and lowering daily motor power use to 108 Wh. These results show that using predictive deep learning along with stochastic control can greatly improve how well photovoltaic tracking systems work and how long they last. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s)},
