	abstract = {The application of artificial intelligence in energy system condition monitoring continues to deepen, with intelligent algorithms offering novel approaches for multi-source data fusion and state perception in photovoltaic (PV) -storage-charging integrated systems. Addressing the challenge of processing multi-modal temporal features—such as PV output, energy storage state of charge (SOC), and charging load—which traditional methods struggle to handle, this study constructs a multi-source data fusion state perception model based on intelligent algorithms. The model utilizes a deep feature extraction network, employing a combined convolutional and recurrent layer encoding to express the temporal dependencies and statistical characteristics of multi-source signals. Building upon this foundation, a multi-head attention fusion mechanism and residual structure are introduced to perform cross-modal feature weighting and dynamic information reconstruction, ultimately forming a high-dimensional state representation vector. Experiments conducted on 6.5×10<sup>6</sup> real-world samples from the Changzhou PV-Storage-Charging Demonstration Station revealed a state recognition accuracy of 98.4%, surpassing the convolutional neural network–long short-term memory (CNN-LSTM) model by 2.2 percentage points. The mean squared error (MSE) was reduced to 0.0041, representing a 31% decrease compared to long short-term memory (LSTM). In edge deployment environments, the average response latency was 42 ms per frame, demonstrating stable performance and real-time adaptability. Results confirm this model effectively integrates multi-source information from PV, energy storage, and loads, significantly enhancing system state recognition accuracy and energy efficiency perception capabilities. It provides technical support for intelligent monitoring and prediction in integrated PV-storage-charging systems. © 2026 SPIE.},
