	abstract = {Increasing the penetration of solar photovoltaic plant (SPVP) in power systems raises issues such as voltage variations and variability in generation. Power system state estimation (SE) is employed to determine the operational state of the existing grid under different operating scenarios. Traditional methods of power system SE use techniques such as the weighted least square method (WLS) and weighted least absolute value (WLAV). These techniques are iterative, computationally inefficient, and sometimes do not converge. Artificial Intelligence (AI)-based tools have increased significantly to enhance the accuracy and efficiency of power system SE. Therefore, this study proposes a feed-forward neural network (FFNN)-based state estimator to execute the SE of a power grid by considering various penetration levels of SPVP. In this study, different deep learning (DL) models have been developed and tested. The proposed state estimator is implemented through MATLAB and validated on IEEE 14 and 30 bus systems. The obtained results show that under the given operating conditions of variable load, generation, and penetration level of the SPVP, the parallel FFNN model outperforms unified FFNN and LSTM. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.},
