	abstract = {As the global energy transition accelerates, the joint forecasting of regional wind power, solar generation, and load demand is critical for enhancing renewable energy utilization, optimizing energy system configurations, and maintaining grid stability. The complex spatiotemporal dynamics and nonlinear interactions among these variables present significant challenges for existing forecasting methods. This study introduces a novel multi-task learning framework TBM (TFT-BiGRU-MultiAttention) that employs shared task experts and Cross-Stitch units to dynamically balance shared and task-specific feature representations. Experimental results demonstrate that the TBM framework achieves R<sup>2</sup> values of 99.227%, 93.958%, and 99.676% for wind power, photovoltaic generation, and load forecasting, respectively, outperforming traditional models such as GRU and MoME. In particular, in load forecasting, the framework records a Mean Absolute Error (MAE) of 57.389 MW and a Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) of 77.700 MW, with substantial improvements over the baseline methods. Additionally, a dropout-based uncertainty quantification approach yields the lowest Continuous Ranked Probability Scores (CRPS) across all tasks, ensuring reliable prediction intervals. Cross-Stitch matrix analysis further reveals task interdependencies effectively captured through adaptive feature sharing, underscoring the framework's robustness and adaptability. These advances provide critical support for the optimization of renewable energy systems and intelligent scheduling. © 2025},
