	affiliations = {Technical Institute of Baqubah, Middle Technical University, Iraq; University of Baghdad, Baghdad, Iraq; Ministry of Higher Education and Scientific Research, Iraq},
	abstract = {The Solar panels are essential elements in the worldwide shift towards sustainable energy. Which are considered as an important source of clean energy. There are many factors that affects its generation capability such as the climate factors. These factors affect the efficacy of solar power systems. Moreover, the geographical placement of solar energy systems significantly influences their functionality. In this work we present a method that predicts solar panel cleaning required. The Physics-Aware Dual-Stream AI (PEDA) architecture is a model that uses a Deep Neural Networks (DNN) and Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) to evaluate soiling severity from solar energy records. In contrast to traditional deep learning methods that depend exclusively on data-driven optimization, PEDA incorporates linear type of Ordinary Differential Equation (ODE), that models soiling rates decline over time, guaranteeing that predictions reduce error while conforming to physical principles. provides the requisite cleaning schedule based on the parameters outlined in the table. The criteria include the dust index, rainfall forecast, and anticipated PR decline. A synthetic dataset made from the Kaggle Solar Panel Soiling data and ERA5 hourly rainfall (Copernicus). The Dust Index was made by binning PM2.5/PM10 (or AOD) and pairing it with simulated operational/meteorological records for parabolic trough collectors. The PEDA model clearly outperforms traditional methods by integrating physical laws directly into its learning process, achieving a Mean Absolute Error (MAE) of 15.7 W/m<sup>2</sup>, Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) of 19.5 W/m<sup>2</sup>, Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) of 4.6%, and an R<sup>2</sup> score of 0.95. Unlike data-driven models—such as the Transformer (MAE: 21.3 W/m<sup>2</sup>) and CNN-LSTM (MAE: 24.1 W/m<sup>2</sup>)—PEDA embeds differential equations in the loss function to model real-world dynamics like dust accumulation and rainfall effects. This enables it to intelligently generate cleaning schedules, reducing unnecessary maintenance and preserving solar panel efficiency even under limited or erratic data conditions. © This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License. License details: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/},
