	pages = {23670 - 23688},
	doi = {10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3663305},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105029850916?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Institute of Science Tokyo, School of Engineering, Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering, Tokyo, 152-8550, Japan; Khon Kaen University, Faculty of Engineering, Department of Electrical Engineering, Khon Kaen, 40002, Thailand; Khon Kaen University, Center for Alternative Energy Research and Development, Khon Kaen, 40002, Thailand},
	abstract = {Recently, a Distribution System (DS) has increasingly integrated multiple MicroGrids (MGs). To exploit MG potential, a DS Operator (DSO) requires an effective energy trading strategy that incentivizes MG Operators (MGOs) to actively exchange energy and preserves MGO benefits. This paper proposes a new energy trading strategy with multiple responses from MGOs. Firstly, a multi-objective optimization problem is performed to create the MGO Pareto front and solved through the proposed optimization framework by combining a multi-agent deep reinforcement learning with random sampling training, a predictive-guide model to generate proper single-objective problems, and a point estimation method to evaluate practical MGO objective values. Secondly, the proposed energy trading strategy allows all MGOs to use their Pareto solutions to respond to the DSO's dynamic buying energy rate, while the DSO is modeled as a DRL agent to learn MGO's multiple responses. Three low-voltage MGs are connected to a medium-voltage DS. Simulation results demonstrate that the proposed optimization framework enables MGOs to generate Pareto solutions with high adaptability to MG uncertainties. Also, MGOs effectively respond to the dynamic buying rate by using their Pareto solutions in the proposed energy trading strategy, while the DSO optimally determines the dynamic buying rate, which can reduce the monthly DSO cost by up to 5.24% compared to applying constant buying rates.  © 2013 IEEE.},
