	affiliations = {Department of Mechanical Engineering, St. Mother Theresa Engineering College, Tamil Nadu, Thoothukudi, India; Department of Mechanical Engineering, RVS Technical Campus, Tamil Nadu, Coimbatore, India; Department of Chemistry, St. Mother Theresa Engineering College, Tamil Nadu, Thoothukudi, India},
	abstract = {This study introduces a novel optimization strategy for roof-type solar stills (SSs) by combining three rarely integrated enhancement methods—flat plate collectors (FPCs), advanced basin configurations (compartmental and corrugated designs with fins), and black limestone as a unique heat storage material. Unlike earlier works that focused on single modifications, this research systematically evaluates their combined effect under varying operational parameters such as water depth and wick material. A key contribution is the integration of machine learning (decision tree and random forest models) with statistical approaches (regression and ANOVA) to predict freshwater yield and identify dominant factors, enabling real-time adaptive optimization. Experimental results showed a maximum productivity of 7800 ml/m²·d, maximum freshwater yield of 5450 ml/m²·d surpassing traditional designs by up to 65 % improvement in yield. The findings reveal that basin liner design and shallow water depth are the most influential factors, validated by both ANOVA and machine learning. This work advances the field by providing a hybrid experimental–data-driven framework for solar desalination, demonstrating how roof-type stills can achieve higher and more consistent freshwater output, particularly in arid regions facing water scarcity. © 2025 The Authors.},
