	abstract = {The work analyzes using a parabolic trough solar concentrator as an energy source for a solar thermal desalination system. The objectives were to develop an energy balance model for a concentrator, calculate its energy capacity, estimate daily desalinated water production, and analyze instrumentation and construction. A parabolic trough concentrator was built with a focal length of 200 mm and an aperture width of 900 mm. Solar tracking was implemented using light-dependent resistors and an Arduino controller. Temperature and solar radiation data were collected using thermocouples and a pyranometer. The concentrator was tested on cloudy and partly cloudy days. Despite clouds, it sometimes reached temperatures over 98 °C and around 900 W/m<sup>2</sup> solar radiation. The estimated daily production capacity was 24.63 L of desalinated water. Average production rates ranged from 68.67 to 82.10 mL/min. The parabolic trough performed well when solar radiation was above 700 W/m<sup>2</sup>. The estimated production capacities demonstrate the concentrator's potential as an energy source for solar desalination systems. © The Author(s), published by EDP Sciences, 2026. This is an Open Access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0), which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.},
