	abstract = {In recent years, satellites have been widely employed in diverse domains such as Earth observation, weather forecasting, and space exploration, generating large-scale data through remote sensing and telemetry. However, these data have primarily been used to analyze surface changes or atmospheric phenomena, while their application to anomaly detection of satellite systems themselves has remained limited. Traditionally, anomaly detection has relied on onboard sensors and ground control centers through Failure Detection, Isolation, and Recovery (FDIR) techniques. Recently, however, increasing attention has been given to detecting anomalous states through cross-observation among multiple cooperative satellites and between satellites and ground stations. In particular, for mission-dedicated satellite constellations such as space-based solar power systems, the necessity of artificial intelligence (AI)-based analysis leveraging remote sensing and telemetry data has been emphasized. This review comprehensively examines recent research trends and representative case studies on AI-based satellite anomaly detection using remote sensing data and provides perspectives on future directions. Copyright © 2025 Korean Society of Remote Sensing | This is an Open Access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution Non-Commercial License (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/) which permits unrestricted non-commercial use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.},
