	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105014733070?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {UMass Amherst, United States; MIT, United States},
	abstract = {Due to declining costs and increasing government incentives, residential rooftop photovoltaic (PV) installations are commonplace in electric grids worldwide. However, despite their potential for deployment on most homes, residential PV installations in the United States are heavily clustered in high-income neighborhoods. In addition to being a widely acknowledged social issue, recent work has shown that these existing installations are highly carbon-ine!cient – that is, they are not optimally situated to effectively offset carbon emissions from traditional fossil fuel-based generators used in the electric grid. Prior work also shows that prioritizing simple metrics such as socioeconomic equity can significantly increase the carbon-e”ciency of new installations. In this paper, we formalize the problem of site selection for rooftop PV installations as a multi-objective optimization problem, where the objectives include metrics of interest such as energy generation, carbon offsetting, and equity along demographic lines. In doing so, we introduce our model, NEAT-Lexicase, which uses a neural network trained via evolutionary learning. We evaluate our model in a case study of the site selection problem using a data set of U.S. solar generation and demographic information, where it improves carbon-e”ciency by 62%, income equity by 41%, and racial equity by 24% while maintaining energy generation comparable to a continuation of current trends. According to recent projections, this optimized placement achieves the estimated carbon offset needed for net-zero emissions (due to energy generation) by 2034, four years earlier than current trends, while also substantially improving both racial and income equity. © 2025 Copyright is held by author/owner(s).},
