	abstract = {Built as a world-class city cluster in China, the Guangdong–Hong Kong–Macao Greater Bay Area (GBA) faces pressure to reconcile rapid economic growth with its carbon peaking commitments. Distributed solar energy, particularly rooftop photovoltaics (RPV), emerges as a strategic solution to reduce carbon emissions while overcoming land constraints. This study proposes an integrated methodology that combines Google Earth Satellite (GES), digital elevation model (DEM), land use and land cover (LULC) map, global tilted irradiation (GTI), and a deep-learning model to assess RPV potential of the GBA. It leverages an advanced deep learning model, RSPrompter, and GES to obtain rooftop segmentation, achieving an unprecedented accuracy of 96.2% that surpasses other models like U-Net, SegNet, HRNet, and DeepLabV3+. Moreover, a shape-based rooftop height estimation by integrating segmented rooftops with DEM and LULC is introduced to enhance the precision of RPV potential assessments. This study reports a total rooftop area of 2428.769 km<sup>2</sup> and an effective rooftop area of 137.547 km<sup>2</sup> in the GBA, capable of generating 11,907.521 GWh of power annually, equivalent to a CO<sub>2</sub> emission reduction of 4,393.875 kilotons. Cities like Guangzhou, Huizhou, and Foshan emerge as top contributors, largely due to their dense industrial clusters. In Shenzhen, industrial and residential rooftops account for 60% of the total RPV potential, while transportation and logistics rooftops exhibit the highest efficiency per rooftop. This study highlights the immense RPV potential in the GBA and showcases the transformative impact of advanced remote sensing technologies in renewable energy planning and policy making. © 2025},
	author_keywords = {Deep learning; Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area; Roof segmentation; Rooftop solar photovoltaic},
	keywords = {China; Foshan; Guangdong; Guangzhou; Huizhou; Shenzhen; carbon dioxide; carbon emission; digital elevation model; emission control; land cover; land use; machine learning; photovoltaic system; power generation; roof; satellite data; segmentation; solar power},
