	affiliations = {South China Normal University, School of Data Science and Engineering, Xingzhi College, Shanwei, 516600, China; Shenzhen Technology University, College of Big Data and Internet, Shenzhen, 518118, China},
	abstract = {Uncrewed aerial vehicles (UAVs) provide an extremely effective solution for environmental monitoring and disaster assessment. Counting tasks are widely applied in UAV-based remote sensing to accurately estimate the number and distribution of objects, particularly in applications such as disaster damage assessment. However, relying solely on counting results limits detailed statistical analysis of specific regions, thereby restricting the potential of downstream tasks. This article proposes a multitask counting and segmentation (MTCS) model that enables concurrent segmentation and counting in a single forward pass, specifically applied to UAV damage assessment. The backbone network, termed the task-agnostic feature network (TAFNet), utilizes nonlearnable primitives (NLPs) to mitigate conflicts between tasks. In addition, we introduce the frequency fusion module (FFM) to replace conventional concatenation methods, thereby enhancing the differentiation of boundary features. We also propose the CBM and SBM to enhance the performance of counting and segmentation tasks, respectively. To enable end-to-end analysis, we introduce the area-specific analysis method (ASAM). Furthermore, we construct a UAV-based remote sensing multitask dataset, PV-CountSeg, which contains data on photovoltaic panels damaged by hail. Extensive experiments conducted on the PV-CountSeg, CARPK, and iSAID datasets demonstrate that MTCS achieves competitive accuracy in counting and segmentation compared to existing single-task models. MTCS is the first multitask model specifically designed for joint segmentation and counting, offering a unified paradigm for downstream tasks in remote sensing regional statistics. © 2008-2012 IEEE.},
