	abstract = {A major challenge in guaranteeing a dependable and efficient electricity supply is the sporadic and fluctuating characteristics of photovoltaic (PV) power output. The use of lithium-ion batteries with advanced energy management systems alleviates fluctuations by storing excess energy during peak production and delivering it during times of low generation or elevated demand. This research presents a novel Energy Management System (EMS) for a grid-connected microgrid that incorporates photovoltaic energy and lithium-ion battery storage. The Grey Wolf Optimizer (GWO) is utilized to optimize the settings of the Fractional-Order Proportional-Integral (FOPI) regulator controlling the system, with results compared with those achieved by Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO). The optimal forecasting method for energy management was determined following an assessment of various predictive techniques, including Autoregressive Integrated Moving Average (ARIMA), Moving Average (MA), Artificial Neural Networks (ANN), Support Vector Machines (SVM), and linear regression. The proposed system, modeled in MATLAB/Simulink and assessed in real-world situations, illustrates the efficacy of the optimized GWO controller. The proposed energy management system enhances state-of-charge (SOC) regulation and voltage stability through the integration of predictive forecasting and advanced control, while also providing a viable solution for extending battery longevity, supporting the grid, and improving the overall reliability of smart PV microgrids. © 2025 School of Science, DUTH. All rights reserved.},
