	year = {2025},
	journal = {IOP Conference Series: Earth and Environmental Science},
	volume = {1560},
	number = {1},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1088/1755-1315/1560/1/012041},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105025436745?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Department of Electrical Engineering, Institute of Sustainable Energy, College of Engineering, Universiti Tenaga Nasional, Malaysia; TNB Renewable Sdn. Bhd, Malaysia},
	abstract = {Nowadays, the used of solar energy to generate power is growing in popularity and is employed globally. Many technologies are used today to harness the energy of the sun such as Photovoltaic (PV) system. PV is one of the fastest growing renewable energy technologies and increasingly becoming an important role towards global energy transformation. Besides, it has a promising potential for building large-scale solar power plants. Solar energy forecasts have a significant impact on decisions related to the operation and management of energy systems. Accurately forecasting solar energy performance is important in ensuring grid reliability, resilience and system risks. Deep learning model is one of the powerful tools especially in forecasting field. A one hour ahead solar energy generation forecasting based on Graph Neural Network (GNN) and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks were proposed and their performances were compared to suggest the reliable and accurate forecasting model for large-scale solar farm. In this study, the evaluation of GNN and LSTM performance were conducted using LSS Sepang database. Four performance metrics were used to assess the model performance, such as root mean squared error (RMSE), mean squared error (MSE), mean absolute error (MAE) and accuracy. The GNN model which uses a self-learning graph structure to represent the relationships between the weather features and solar energy generation showed a superior performance with MSE (0.17), RMSE (0.41), MAE (1.1) and an accuracy of 90.30%. However, the LSTM model designed for time-series data achieved MSE (0.21), RMSE (0.45), MAE (1.33) and an accuracy of 81.36%. These results indicate that the GNN model outperforms the LSTM model in terms of accuracy and error metrics which demonstrating GNN model potential as an effective tool for forecasting solar energy generation in large-scale solar farms. This study highlights the results of GNN and LSTM for this application and suggesting that GNN model provides more reliable and accurate forecasting which can help improving the efficiency of solar energy systems. © Published under licence by IOP Publishing Ltd.},
