	abstract = {Effective energy policies and measures are critical in advancing a country's sustainable development and enhancing the quality of life. This is especially important in today's world, where the greenhouse effect poses a significant threat. Countries across the globe are striving to improve energy efficiency, and Taiwan is no exception. Among the most pressing challenges are reducing carbon emissions and accelerating the adoption of green, renewable energy. A key solution lies in increasing the power output of photovoltaic systems. However, meteorological conditions and environmental factors heavily influence photovoltaic power generation, leading to high variability and intermittency. These characteristics pose challenges to the safe and stable operation of the power grid. To address these issues, accurately predicting photovoltaic power generation is essential. This can help mitigate the negative impacts of integrating photovoltaics into the grid and support the stable operation of the power system. This paper introduces an innovative and effective solution to this problem by developing a short-term photovoltaic power prediction model. The model Bi-directional Quantum Long Short-term Memory (Bi-QLSTM) is a QLSTM neural network capable of processing sequence data in both directions. The model is applied to short-term photovoltaic power prediction, and real-world examples demonstrate its ability to enhance prediction accuracy significantly. This makes it highly suitable for deployment and scaling within Taiwan's domestic power system.  © Published under licence by IOP Publishing Ltd.},
