	abstract = {Renewables have grown fastly in the last decades, significantly contributing to GreenHouse Gas (GHG) emission reduction due to the use of green sources and local energy production/consumption. However, the intermittent nature of renewable sources and the sudden variations in load demand require optimal energy management to efficiently coordinate local energy flows. In this context, the design of a suitable Energy Management System (EMS) and an appropriate Energy Storage System (ESS) plays a key role in supporting the reliance of renewables, as well as their better exploitation. This paper proposes a "smart"energy management strategy for a grid-connected Photovoltaic (PV) system, equipped with an ESS, installed in a Smart Building. The algorithm regulates power flows based on predictions made by Artificial Neural Networks (ANN). The goals are i) not to overstress the energy storage system, ii) to increase the operational lifetime of the batteries, and iii) meet the dynamic load demand. The methodology will be then validated through experimental data, which will be collected from the PV plant installed in ENEA Research Center, located in Casaccia, Rome (Italy). © Published under licence by IOP Publishing Ltd.},
