	affiliations = {Institute of Computer Science, University of Bern, Bern, CH-3012, Switzerland; Institute of System Engineering, University of Applied Sciences and Arts Western Switzerland, Sion, Switzerland},
	abstract = {Accurate photovoltaic (PV) power modeling and prediction is vital for both optimizing energy management and ensuring grid stability in renewable energy systems. In the present paper, we explore and compare the performance of various regression-based and data-driven models for PV power estimation and prediction, applied to the same plant over identical periods. In particular, we research and compare the following models: polynomial regression, autoregressive with exogenous inputs (ARX), nonlinear ARX (NARX), and long short-term memory (LSTM). The empirical results reveal that while simpler models like polynomial regression and ARX are efficient and straightforward to interpret, they are limited in handling nonlinearities and long-term dependencies. On the other hand, the NARX model with neural networks demonstrates improved predictive capabilities for dynamic systems. LSTM, leveraging both climate data and historical PV power, achieves superior accuracy for shorter prediction horizons compared with the other models, but has high computational complexity. This paper provides practical insights into selecting predictive and estimative models based on specific requirements, highlighting the trade-offs between simplicity and accuracy.  © 2025 IEEE.},
