	abstract = {With the growing significance of renewable energy in the global energy structure, accurate photovoltaic power prediction is crucial for efficient power grid management. However, real-world photo- voltaic data is inherently nonlinear and non-stationary, posing challenges for precise prediction. This paper proposes a hybrid model, PatchTST-DFT-SO, which integrates discrete fourier transform decomposition with the novel patch time series transformer model, where the parameters are optimized using the snake optimization algorithm. Discrete Fourier transform is applied to fixed-length, three-day input segments to separate seasonal and trend-cyclical components, reducing the complexity of photovoltaic data modeling. Patch time series transformer model is employed for further predictions of these components, while the snake optimization algorithm optimizes the parameters in embedding module to adapt to diverse datasets. Extensive experiments are conducted on the Desert Knowledge Australia Solar Centre Dataset, comparing the results with those of previously proposed methods. The results demonstrate that the proposed model achieves a 40.4% reduction in RMSE compared with previous methods. According to the ablation study, PatchTST-DFT-SO achieves up to a 17.4% reduction in RMSE relative to the original patch time series transformer model. The proposed model achieves an inference speed of 21.4 ms per prediction on an NVIDIA Jetson TX2 edge device, satisfying the industry-standard real-time threshold of 1min for grid-tied PV systems.  © 2025 The Authors.},
