	abstract = {Seasonality-based feature selection encompasses categorizing and picking features that reflect seasonal changes in data. For Global Horizontal Irradiance (GHI) prediction of Stand-Alone Photovoltaic (SAPV) system, this necessitates focusing on variables that demonstrate substantial seasonal patterns. Intensity of solar radiation fluctuates all through the year owing to the Earth's tilt and meteorological conditions. Machine Learning (ML) models can generate precise and reliable GHI forecasts by choosing features that capture such seasonal variations, therefore boosting the performance of SAPV systems. The primary focus of this work involves acquiring the meteorological dataset covering four climatically diverse Indian locations. Kurtosis and Skewness meters suggests normal data distribution, but further tests directed using Cramer-von Mises, Anderson-Darling and Kolmogorov-Smirnov tests indicates non-normality of the data distribution. Consequently, non-parametric relevance measures, including Spearman's, Hoeffding's and Kendall's coefficients are used to select significant features for accurate seasonal GHI prediction. Features with weak or no correlation and low mutual information are eliminated, resulting in the selection of nine relevant features. These features are meticulously trained and tested using four ML models. Performance is evaluated based on error metrics revealing that Gaussian Process Regression model outperformed others in various Indian climatic conditions, while Ensemble of Trees model was less effective across all conditions. © 2025 The Author(s)},
