	affiliations = {Parul University, Vadodara, India},
	abstract = {As the deployment and installation of photovoltaic (PV) systems continue to rise at a significant pace, the importance of implementing effective fault detection mechanisms has become increasingly crucial. This is essential to ensure that these systems operate at their optimal performance levels and maintain their longevity. In response to this need, a comprehensive study has been conducted that integrates the advanced techniques of machine learning (ML) with the precision of thermal imaging technology. The aim of this research is to pinpoint and identify a variety of issues that can affect PV systems, including inverter failures, array discrepancies, and the influence of environmental factors. The study has successfully developed sophisticated models using machine learning algorithms, specifically the Random Forest and Support Vector Machine (SVM) models. These models have been trained on a dataset comprising current, voltage, and phase characteristics of PV systems. Remarkably, the Random Forest model achieved an accuracy of 99.75%, while the SVM model pushed the boundaries further with an accuracy of 99.92%. These impressive figures underscore the reliability and precision of the machine learning models in detecting faults within PV systems. In addition to the machine learning models, thermal imaging technology has been employed as a complementary tool to enhance the fault detection process. Thermal cameras are utilized to capture infrared images rapidly, which is particularly useful for identifying defects and issues within the PV systems that are not discernible to the naked eye. This thermal imaging approach adds a layer of detail and immediacy to the fault detection process, making it possible to visualize and address problems that might otherwise go unnoticed until they cause significant damage or performance degradation. The integration of machine learning models and thermal imaging technology presents a robust and innovative method for improving the real-time observation capabilities of PV systems. This approach not only facilitates the early detection of potential faults but also enables anticipatory maintenance, which is a proactive strategy aimed at preventing system failures before they occur. By predicting and addressing issues in advance, the overall dependability and efficiency of PV systems are significantly enhanced. Copyright © the author(s). This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License.},
