	abstract = {This study focuses on the development and comparison of predictive models for solar energy generation and building energy consumption in Singapore, examining the causality between urban morphology and these objectives. Two modeling techniques were employed: Multiple Linear Regression (MLR) and Artificial Neural Networks (ANNs). Both MLR and ANN models were developed through extensive tuning and testing of hyperparameters and parameters. The primary aim was to explore the relationship between urban morphological characteristics and two key aspects: solar energy potential and building energy consumption, and to compare the performance of the two models. 720 neighborhood-scale simulations using Rhino-Grasshopper Ladybug and Honeybee were conducted to generate data for model development. Results showed that ANN models consistently outperformed MLR models across performance metrics. The ANN models achieved an R-squared value of 0.95 for rooftop electricity generation, compared to 0.91 for LR models. For facade electricity generation, LR models showed performance degradation from training (R<sup>2</sup> = 0.83) to validation (R<sup>2</sup> = 0.74), while ANN models maintained stability with a validation R<sup>2</sup> of 0.93. Sensitivity analysis identified compactness and sky view factor as critical parameters influencing energy performance, with compactness contributing 32% to cooling load variance and sky view factor (rooftop) accounting for 77.4% of the variance in rooftop PV generation. These results highlight the importance of selecting appropriate modeling techniques tailored to urban energy planning needs and demonstrate the better predictive accuracy of ANN models in capturing complex urban morphological interactions. © 2025 Building Simulation Conference Proceedings. All rights reserved.},
