	doi = {10.3233/FAIA231322},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85192437591?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {NARI Technology Co. Ltd, Nanjing, 211106, China},
	abstract = {In actual operation, photovoltaic electric fields often encounter the problem of missing photoelectric output power. In order to ensure the continuity, timing, and integrity of the optoelectronic power data samples, improve the efficiency of optoelectronic data analysis and mining work, and improve the accuracy of optoelectronic power prediction, it is necessary to reconstruct the missing data of the optoelectronic unit. This article adopts a deep learning-based bidirectional interpolation algorithm for photovoltaic power, starting from both temporal and spatial dimensions, to explore the temporal and spatial dependencies of photovoltaic electric field station-related data and establish corresponding spatiotemporal models. Finally, the rationality of step selection was determined through time-step analysis experiments. When the time step is 4, the predicted power error is 10.43%, and the power interpolation curve has the best fit with the measured power curve. Through bidirectional interpolation experiments, the rationality and effectiveness of the bidirectional interpolation theory in supplementing missing data of photoelectric power were verified. The missing data on photoelectric power is affected by existing data before and after the time period. When the forward and reverse weights are 0.6 and 0.4, respectively, the predicted power error is 9.64%. Through comparative experiments, the power prediction error of the model in this paper is 10.43%, verifying the superiority of the model compared to the other three neural network models in predicting the error of optoelectronic power.  © 2024 The Authors.},
