	abstract = {The photovoltaic inverter serves as the core component of solar photovoltaic power generation equipment. However, during its operational lifecycle, common issues such as aging of the IGBT on the inverter's DC side often arise. This aging not only hinders the inverter's output power and efficiency but also undermines the stable operation of the entire photovoltaic power generation system. In this paper, to address the aging faults of DC-side IGBTs in PV inverters, a novel approach utilizing the Long- and Short-term Time-series Network (LSTNet) is introduced for aging fault prediction, which employs the transient turn-off spike voltage as a key characteristic parameter. Additionally, the Mini-Batch Gradient Descent (MBGT) method is employed to optimize the model, enabling more accurate predictions of aging faults and improved handling of noisy data. By utilizing the accelerated aging dataset for IGBTs provided by the NASA test center, the model is constructed and subjected to rigorous testing for comprehensive evaluation. By analyzing the experimental results for the comparative analysis of the model error, compared with the recurrent neural network and wavelet neural network prediction model, the MBGD-LSTNet network model exhibits reduced error and enhanced prediction accuracy, significantly improving the precision of forecasting aging faults in IGBTs. © Published under licence by IOP Publishing Ltd.},
