	abstract = {Cloud forecast information is important in many applications like solar power planning, optical satellite acquisition, and weather study. The Numerical Weather Prediction (NWP) model is a powerful tool to predict future cloud occurrences. However, the model needs hardware resources with high performance and capacity to result in high spatial resolution. The complexity of the forecasting model can be reduced by using a data-driven method (machine learning or deep learning) instead of the NWP model. On the other hand, remote sensing data is a good source of data for making predictions over large areas. The current forecasting model, which utilizes remote sensing data and data-driven methods, results in very short-term forecasting because the model relies on cloud development. This research introduces the use of parameters from the ground surface and low atmosphere to create a longer-term forecasting model. The method combines statistical methods on temporal data and spatial interpolation to estimate the values of surface parameters under clouds. Moreover, data reconstruction is implemented in the basic Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) architecture. The model uses temporal data of aggregated-interpolated spectral values from Himawari-8 satellite data, which relate to the process of cloud formation. The model results in monthly forecasting, simulating cloud occurrence probability from February to July 2020 with a good accuracy of less than 10% Normalized RMSE (NRMSE). © 2024 The Authors.},
	author_keywords = {Cloud forecast; Convolutional Neural Network; Himawari-8; remote sensing},
	keywords = {Cloud forecasts; Convolutional neural network; Data-driven methods; Forecast information; Forecasting models; Himawari-8; Numerical weather prediction models; Remote sensing data; Remote-sensing; Temporal Data; Convolutional neural networks},
