	affiliations = {PROMES-CNRS (UPR 8521), Rambla de la thermodynamique, Tecnosud, Perpignan, 66100, France; PROMES-CNRS (UPR 8521), 7 rue du four solaire, Font-Romeu, Odeillo, 66120, France; University of Perpignan Via Domitia, 52 Avenue Paul Alduy, Perpignan, 66860, France},
	abstract = {Short-term forecasting of direct normal irradiance (DNI), which refers to photons that did not interact with the atmosphere on their way to the observer, is of great interest in the advanced management of concentrated solar power systems. So, this paper exhibits a hybrid model dedicated to the intrahour forecasting of DNI (horizons are 5, 10, and 15 min). This hybrid model combines a knowledge-based model with a machine learning model: the knowledge-based model is used for clear-sky DNI forecasting from DNI measurements; the machine learning model evaluates the impact of the atmospheric disturbances on the solar resource, through the processing of high dynamic range sky images provided by a ground-based camera. The hybrid model is evaluated by comparing its performance, for different sky conditions, with that of two machine learning models based on past DNI observations only. The results highlight the pertinence of combining knowledge-based models with data-driven models, and of integrating sky-imaging data in the DNI forecasting process: the proposed hybrid model successfully manages clear-sky, overcast, and mixed situations. Regardless of the forecast horizon, the hybrid model outperforms the reference models both in normalized root mean squared error and in DNI ramp detection. © 2022 International Solar Energy Society},
