	abstract = {Solar panels generate energy by utilizing the sun rays on their surface, which depends on the amount of surface temperature and the strength of solar radiation. To maximize the energy conversion efficiency, the solar PV panel should operate at maximum power point (MPP). Each maximum power point tracking (MPPT) method has its unique conversion efficacy and tracking strategy of MPP. This paper describes a novel approach to operating the PV system at MPP by implementing linear and nonlinear regression-type machine learning algorithms. The data acquired from the specifications of the PV panel was used to train, validate, and test the machine learning models. These algorithms predict the maximum available power at the PV panel and its corresponding voltage for specific quantities of irradiation and temperature. These predicted values help determine the boost converter's duty cycle to work the system at the predicted MPP by regression machine learning models. The simulation results show that regression algorithms forced the PV panel to work at the predicted MPP even in the presence of changes in climatic conditions regarding solar radiation and temperature. The PV system has an efficiency higher than 95.21% with linear regression models and more than 95.32% with nonlinear regression models. The simulation results are compared with the beta MPPT and ANN approaches, which were existing MPPT methods in the literature, to show the efficacy of the proposed regression machine learning strategy © 2022, International Journal of Renewable Energy Research.All Rights Reserved.},
	author_keywords = {Boost converter; Linear and nonlinear regression; Maximum power point tracking; Photovoltaic system; Regression machine learning},
	keywords = {Boost converter; Energy conversion efficiency; Learning algorithms; Machine learning; Maximum power point trackers; Regression analysis; Solar radiation; BOOST converter; Linear and nonlinear regression; Machine learning algorithms; Machine-learning; Maximum power point; Maximum Power Point Tracking; Non-linear regression; Photovoltaic systems; PV panel; Regression machine learning; Solar panels},
